WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:16.750
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.750
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.289
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:23.609
My guest today, I'm so excited to have with
us, Todd McFarlane.

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:27.220
He is an Emmy- and Grammy-winning director,
producer, and creator of one of the world's

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.150
best-selling comic books, Spawn.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.540
He is best known to many comic book fans for
his work as the artist on The Amazing Spider-Man,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.370
for which he co-created Marvel's top villain,
Venom.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.100
Todd is the CEO of Todd McFarlane Productions,
McFarlane Toys, one of the US' top action

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.820
figure manufacturers, and McFarlane Films.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:50.351
He is also a co-founder of Image Comics, which
debuted Spawn in 1992, selling 1.7 million

00:00:50.351 --> 00:00:52.300
copies of the first issue.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.719
In 1997, Spawn was made into an Emmy award-winning
animated series on HBO and a live-action feature

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:00.870
film that grossed more than $100 million.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.559
In 2019.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:07.640
Todd made history with Spawn #301, earning
the Guinness World Record for longest trying

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.760
creator-owned superhero comic books series.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.280
You can find him on all the social Instagram
@toddmcfarlane, Twitter @Todd_ McFarlane,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.280
Facebook liketoddmcfarlane.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.180
Todd, welcome to the show.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.850
Nice to see you.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:19.850
Todd McFarlane: Hey, Tim.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.720
Thanks for having me this morning.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.720
Appreciate it.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.710
Tim Ferriss: And I thought we could start
with a confession on my side, which is ever

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:32.470
since I was a kid and to this day, I still
have a poster of The Incredible Hulk #340,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.250
Grey Hulk versus Wolverine, that features
your artwork.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.390
And I am a longtime collector and fan of your
work.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.450
So it's exciting to have you here and I'm
excited to dig into all sorts of things.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:52.060
And I thought we would begin, and this might
be a dead end, we'll see where it goes, but

00:01:52.060 --> 00:02:01.751
asking about how baseball informed your approach
to art and comics, if at all, if those two

00:02:01.751 --> 00:02:02.979
things tie together for you.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.329
Todd McFarlane: So here's what I would say
about that.

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:07.759
And I'll give you a little bit of back history.

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:15.701
I would say it informed me more on the eventual
business side, the competitiveness on the

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.030
business side.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.330
It's always interesting when you do certain
interviews with people.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:28.680
I always think that sometimes interviews are
laying on a psychologist's couch, and they're

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.450
like, "Todd, why are you like that?

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.900
What drives you?"

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.709
Here's the answer to that question so we can
get that out of the way.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.760
I think it was the day I came out of the womb
and it was in my DNA, right?

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.050
So there's not a single day I don't recall
not being taught.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.580
So what is natural for me, I guess for other
people, because I now understand as I get

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.960
older, their personalities, that my personality
has been baked every day of my life.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:01.950
And it's like it's not an effort to do what
I've done in life.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.290
So people go, "Oh, my God, you're so tenacious,
and you go up against people, and you're such

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.730
a rebel, and you fight for what you believe
in."

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.730
Of course.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.900
There's no other option in my brain.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.010
So I'm not fighting, there's no other option.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.780
So it's just a natural progression on where
I want to go.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:25.159
But I would argue that whatever that DNA is
got enhanced with two things.

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:29.989
One, I had a brother that was a year older
and one a year younger.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:32.630
And then you get three boys together, what
are you talking about?

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:36.950
Every day was a competition, who could eat
the cereal the fastest, who could jump the

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.980
most steps down, who could run to school the
fast — what are you talking about?

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.120
Everything is a competition.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.520
And then you take that and eventually —
I was never going to go to university.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.970
I remember being in class when they brought
— in high school, they brought the recruit

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.030
into college, and they sort of talked.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.390
And I go, "Well, I'm not going, so I'll just
put my head down and continue drawing," because

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.599
I was always doodling.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:05.430
And then somebody tapped me on the shoulder,
I remember, and they went, "Son, why is your

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.780
arm not raised?"

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:07.810
And I'm like, "What?"

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.250
I wasn't even paying attention, sadly.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:12.030
And I go, "Well, what was the question?"

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.900
They go, "Well, who wants to go to college?"

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:19.690
And I looked up and every single person in
my class had their arm up.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.570
Now that's okay.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:26.390
But I looked at the two sort of druggies that
I know because I'm friends with all of them.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.570
And I'm looking at them going, "What are you
talking about?

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:32.640
You're not going to college, You got Fs across
the board.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.560
You might even drop out in grade 12."

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:41.750
So anyways, I gave them my reason, which is
I don't enjoy education.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.930
And so let's just convert it to broccoli,
and let's say I don't like broccoli.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.919
Why would I then go and pay people money to
eat more broccoli?

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:52.860
Doesn't make much sense, does it?

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.479
So I go, "I'm not going."

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:55.669
Now, did I go to college?

00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:57.010
Of course, I did.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.010
Why?

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.340
Because I'm an athlete, and so I played baseball.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.250
Getting back to your question.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:07.970
And somebody offered me a scholarship to go
to play baseball.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:12.460
So my last three years, I was on a Pac-10
baseball scholarship.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.550
And at some point, now you have to get sort
of simplistic.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.780
If somebody's going to give you free education,
I'm going to grab it.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:20.800
Did I want it?

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:21.800
Not really.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.040
But if it's free, I'm going to grab it.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:31.419
And oddly, Tim, of the 25 guys on our team,
only two of us graduated with a degree in

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:33.340
four years.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.570
And the other guy was — I'm Canadian.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:35.570
The other guy was a Canadian.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:39.990
I remember the coach going, "Because Canadians
don't want to hustle as much in sports."

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:40.990
Right?

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:41.990
Because we got a degree?

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:42.990
What are you talking about?

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.319
It was free.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:50.080
And the reason I took it and I got it done
for free because the dumb athlete was alive

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.060
and well, but they're not really dumb, they
just don't go to class.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.220
And if you don't go to class, you don't get
your marks.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.930
And if you don't get your marks, then eventually
you don't get your degree.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.750
Here's the funny thing about that.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:06.449
Somebody is offering you a free degree, and
23 of my teammates decided they wouldn't get

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:07.810
it in four years.

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:13.440
So either they would never get a degree, or,
gun to my head, this is weird, they're going

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.050
to come back and pay to finish it.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.580
I go, "Finish it?

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:22.110
The buffet was opened the whole time you were
there and you chose not to eat."

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.370
Oh, my gosh.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.830
So if nothing else, given that I'm sort of
a cheap skate, I'm going, "They're going to

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:28.830
give it to me for free.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.500
I'm not paying another penny to come back
here, I'll take that degree."

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.650
So I got my degree and I was off to the races.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:40.480
Now, between the baseball, which is competitive,
and my brothers, then all of a sudden, three

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.660
weeks before I graduate, I end up getting
my first job in comic books.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:52.720
And all of that sort of made me a freelancer,
and I had to begin taking care of myself.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.020
And it's like, "Okay, just another game, another
game of competition."

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:01.009
Tim Ferriss: First job in comic books, you
have posted, I want to say, photographs of

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:02.639
350 or so, rejection letters.

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:03.639
Todd McFarlane: Yep.

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:06.810
Tim Ferriss: When did you first start sending
those out?

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:13.169
And did you get any particularly helpful feedback
that allowed you to modify things so that

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:14.669
you were able to get that first job?

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:15.669
Todd McFarlane: Yes.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:18.090
So let's go through it real quick.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.169
Did I get over 300 rejections?

00:07:21.169 --> 00:07:22.169
Yes.

00:07:22.169 --> 00:07:23.169
Is that tenacious?

00:07:23.169 --> 00:07:27.180
Is that determination, or is that delusion?

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:31.750
At what point do you say, "I'm going to be
an opera singer," and people keep giving you

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.780
no's, and you go, "Man, look at how determined..."

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.560
At what point do you say, "Maybe I just can't
sing opera?"

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:37.560
Right?

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:38.560
But again —
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:39.560
Todd McFarlane: So there's a fine line.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.750
People give me way too much credit for those
300.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:48.370
I think a normal sane person with sort of
less enthusiasm, we'll leave it at that word,

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:53.539
than me would've probably, at 200 rejections,
found another option to make money.

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:58.400
But the reason I was able to assimilate that
many rejections was because I was going to

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.430
college.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:05.360
So I was sending off samples almost continuously
while I was in school.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.139
So it's like I didn't have a job, I was going
to school, so I didn't care.

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:10.420
I had four years to basically try and get
a job.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.610
And then probably at the end of those four
years, I was going to look at that pile and

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.560
say, "Maybe I need to find something else."

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:22.770
My degree, I thought, is in graphic designing,
and I thought I was going to be the guy who's

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.610
going to do Michelin Tire ads, right?

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:25.610
I go, "That's okay.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:28.539
It's an admirable job, it's a graphic designer."

00:08:28.539 --> 00:08:32.860
That was sort of where I thought the reality
of it was going to be.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.380
But three weeks before I graduate, I get my
first job.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:37.380
And how did I get it?

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:43.560
By sending literally 700 samples over the
course of those four years.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:49.970
And just on one level, I think I just wore
them out because I sent it to every editor

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.230
at every company.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:56.640
And so they used to have, let's say at Marvel,
because my first job was at Marvel, they have

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:57.700
one submission editor.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.920
No, no, no, no, no.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.660
The people who give you the work are the editors,
and they have 16 editors.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.440
So I would send it to all 16 editors.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.040
Ultimately, I went around the submission guy,
I just went, "What?

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.029
You've got to give it to the editors anyways.

00:09:12.029 --> 00:09:14.000
Might as well send it directly to the editors."

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.951
So I would keep sending it to the editors
over and over and over to every company, every

00:09:18.951 --> 00:09:19.951
editor.

00:09:19.951 --> 00:09:25.110
And I think, Tim, in hindsight, I think that
they probably had a board meeting or whatever,

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:28.770
one of those Monday morning meetings, and
they just said something like, and I'm making

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:35.709
this up, but said, "Oh, for the love of God,
that Todd kid, that punk that keeps sending

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:39.740
us, we keep getting a box of mail from this
guy every two weeks.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:44.829
Would somebody in this room give him a couple
pages just so we don't have to open up his

00:09:44.829 --> 00:09:46.279
mail every two weeks?"

00:09:46.279 --> 00:09:49.530
I think I just wore 'em out.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.770
And I got the job literally three weeks before
I graduated.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.270
So I never even had to use my degree.

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:56.730
What informed me in those?

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:57.730
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.600
Todd McFarlane: It was all constructive criticism.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.120
Let me tell you, because people say, "Oh,
Todd, you got the last laugh."

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.290
No, no, no.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.019
Everything they put in those letters was constructive
criticism.

00:10:08.019 --> 00:10:12.730
Because the people who just thought that I
was not very good threw my portfolio and my

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.269
samples in the garbage.

00:10:14.269 --> 00:10:22.350
So everybody who took the time to write back
actually gave a little bit of insight.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:27.839
And so what I would do that didn't know was
actually going to keep me fueled, is that

00:10:27.839 --> 00:10:33.430
I would take that insight and then redo another
batch and send it to everybody again.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:40.530
So where I was making 20 mistakes, eventually
it got down to 18 and then 16 and then 15.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:46.220
And I think probably when I was at six mistakes,
I think they finally said, "Hey, he's getting

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:47.220
better.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.420
He's not perfect.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:50.680
And he seems to be enthusiastic.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.720
So somebody give him a chance, see what he
can do."

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:58.770
Tim Ferriss: So for people who don't have
any familiarity with comic books, penciling,

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:03.010
anything along these lines, what were some
of the things you were getting feedback on

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.740
and getting better at?

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:10.110
And I suppose this leads into the question
of, what does it look like as a comic book

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.110
artist to get better?

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.860
What are you getting better at?

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.389
Maybe that sounds like a silly question, but
I'll give it a shot.

00:11:15.389 --> 00:11:19.641
Todd McFarlane: There's two things that I
think make a good artist in the comic book

00:11:19.641 --> 00:11:21.010
industry.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:24.800
One is just pure drawing skills.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:31.660
So there are hundreds if not thousands of
people who can draw circles around me.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.769
So that if you can just draw pretty pictures,
you can go a long way.

00:11:36.769 --> 00:11:41.160
The second piece is storytelling.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.490
And if you can do great storytelling and be
an average drawer, you're still going to have

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:53.450
a pretty good career because people then will
be entertained by everything you do.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:58.240
So I'll use an example, and because I'm friends
with him, I hope he doesn't take it —

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.579
Frank Miller is a great, great storyteller.

00:12:01.579 --> 00:12:04.030
He's the one that wrote The Dark Knight for
Batman.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:09.100
He's the one that created 300 that ended up
getting turned into a movie, and Sin City

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.779
and all these things.

00:12:10.779 --> 00:12:14.690
He rejuvenated dead characters like Daredevil.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:21.430
I wouldn't say, and I think Frank would agree,
that he's not the best anatomically correct

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:25.610
artist and his drawings don't get every muscle
right.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:32.460
What he does is he tells stories better than
anybody else in our industry, period.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:37.910
So he could do it with stick men, and you
would still be engaged because of his writing,

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:41.620
the way he does it, and what's happening in
it.

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:47.000
So Frank has been able to take that storytelling,
which to me as I've gotten older, is way more

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.899
important than whether you've got flashy lines.

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:53.210
I came in as a kid who wanted to do flashy
lines because people go, "Oh, my God, look

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:54.670
at all the detail he's doing."

00:12:54.670 --> 00:13:01.630
But then I found out that really what they
wanted was less flash and more clarity on

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:02.630
the pages.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:09.000
You should be able to give a comic book to
a non-comic book writer, go next door to your

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.570
neighbor, give it to your mom.

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:15.089
And at the end, they should be able to say,
"Not my cup of tea.

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:18.550
I don't collect comic books, but that was
kind of interesting," because they understood

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:22.639
what they were supposed to be reading in the
sequence they were supposed to be reading

00:13:22.639 --> 00:13:23.639
it.

00:13:23.639 --> 00:13:26.029
And if you don't do that, you're no good in
our industry.

00:13:26.029 --> 00:13:31.649
One more thing, if you can't keep a deadline,
then you're for sure not good at anything.

00:13:31.649 --> 00:13:36.649
As a matter of fact, people who can keep a
deadline in an industry that is driven by

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:44.209
monthly deadlines can have long careers and
not be very good at drawing because you have

00:13:44.209 --> 00:13:47.220
to get product out every 30 days.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:51.769
So go ahead if you want to be the kid that's
flashy and do a bunch of lines and take twice

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:56.620
as long, but they're never going to give you
a regular gig because you have to get books

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:58.920
out on time, period, out.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.400
Tim Ferriss: And I'm embarrassed that I don't
know this, but I never made it far enough.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:03.220
Todd McFarlane: You can still lead a productive
life, Tim.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:04.220
Don't worry about that.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:07.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm working on it, working
on the productive side.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:10.120
But what are the deliverables on a monthly
basis?

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.990
Are you shipping out a few pages at a time?

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:17.560
Are they waiting until you have the entire
book done, so to speak?

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:23.079
What do the actual deadlines and deliverables
look like for a full-time comic book artist?

00:14:23.079 --> 00:14:26.910
Todd McFarlane: So it has shifted, as you
can imagine, with technology.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.500
So the way that it used to work, and I'll
age myself because I got into the business

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:38.630
sort of pre-internet, that I'd have to do
my pages, take them to the — either phone

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:42.790
FedEx — and they were called Federal Express
at that point.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:45.360
And so I would have to phone Federal Express.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.550
When I use FedEx, my wife goes, "Oh, you're
so hip, calling them FedEx."

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.800
And then eventually they caught up.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.690
But so you'd phone FedEx, they'd either come
and pick it up.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.130
Or sometimes you would miss the call on their
deadline.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:03.160
The drivers were — I was living in a remote
area up in Canada and on an island in Canada.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:09.550
And so if I missed a driver, I would literally
— which is why I hired an assistant to help

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:10.800
sort of package stuff up.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:15.290
He would drive to the airport, which was about
an hour and 15 away, while I was finishing

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:16.290
up pages.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.120
So I go, "Oh, I've got another hour."

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.769
And then we drive to the airport.

00:15:21.769 --> 00:15:23.620
Do you know how many times I ran down the
tarmac?

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:29.019
Because it was a little sort of prop plane
that flew to Vancouver, British Columbia.

00:15:29.019 --> 00:15:32.240
I think he was always looking over his shoulder
going, "Todd will be here in about two minutes."

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:37.600
And I'd be running down the tarmac, and I'd
basically throw my package to him, and he'd

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:38.600
get it done.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:44.660
Today, all of that's taken away because now
you can scan your pages and hit, literally,

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:45.800
a download.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:47.410
Boom, it's gone.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:49.460
It doesn't solve anything, Tim.

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:54.850
All's it means is you just get to push your
luck with deadlines later and later.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:57.930
So let's give you an example currently happening.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:03.160
The biggest comic book that's going to come
out this year in our industry is a book called

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:05.730
Batman/Spawn, Right?

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:11.490
That goes to print — because I just talked
to the people at DC Comics yesterday.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:13.670
It goes to print on Monday, right?

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.300
You and I are talking on Friday, right?

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:16.300
Tim Ferriss: Friday.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:19.019
Todd McFarlane: I still have to write — it's
a 48-page book.

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:20.160
I've only written eight pages.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:22.560
I've got to finish writing the 40 pages.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:25.190
There's 10 pages that haven't been colored.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:31.240
I literally talked to the person who does
the word balloons after I give them the script

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:36.050
to say, "Hey, sorry, dude, we've got to both
work pretty hard over the weekend."

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:39.870
They're going to probably get the last pages
at midnight on Sunday.

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:43.829
They're going to look at it, make sure no
pages are upside down or backwards.

00:16:43.829 --> 00:16:46.420
And then they're going to hit send, and it's
going to go to the printer.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.962
And the printer's waiting because when you've
got a big print run — because like I said,

00:16:49.962 --> 00:16:53.780
it's going to be the biggest of this year.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:57.089
You can't swap out books and say, "Ah, we'll
just swap out another book.

00:16:57.089 --> 00:16:58.310
Can you just substitute?"

00:16:58.310 --> 00:16:59.529
Not of that magnitude.

00:16:59.529 --> 00:17:04.780
They've got lots of printer presses waiting
for this one book, and we've got to deliver.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.309
So how does it work?

00:17:06.309 --> 00:17:09.190
By our chinny chin chin, like a lot of other
things in the world.

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:10.309
You just get it done.

00:17:10.309 --> 00:17:15.300
Tim Ferriss: Well, so let me back up for a
second because I think I heard you correctly.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:19.870
Now, you have delivered so many deadlines,
even if you have to chase down the plane on

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:20.870
the tarmac.

00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:21.870
You know how to train, obviously, through
the athletics.

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.140
Todd McFarlane: I missed the plane a couple
times.

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:24.910
Tim Ferriss: You've missed the plane a couple
times.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:29.450
So this is a huge book, biggest in the industry
of this year.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:32.450
Did I hear you correctly that you said you
have eight pages done out of 48?

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:34.090
I guess I'm just wondering if you —
Todd McFarlane: Writing-wise.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:35.090
Tim Ferriss: What does that mean?

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:36.090
Yeah, writing-wise.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.090
Okay.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:38.090
Todd McFarlane: That means —
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:39.090
So is it —
Todd McFarlane: Tim, I'll tell you what that

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:40.090
means.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:43.270
That means before I talked to you this morning,
I talked to what they call the letterer, the

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:46.880
guy who actually converts your script into
the word balloon.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:51.710
I talk to my letterer and go, "Dude, we're
going all night tonight.

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:53.690
Every three pages I'm going to send to you."

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:56.370
So it's going to be, I do three, I send it
to him, he works on it.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:59.970
By the time he's done with those three, I
feed him another three, and we're just going

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:03.760
to see how it works and we'll get it done.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:04.890
Tim, we'll get it done.

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:05.890
Right?

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:06.890
Like you said earlier —
Tim Ferriss: I believe you.

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:08.490
Todd McFarlane: — a couple years ago, I
set a record.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:11.340
I mean, Spawn is the longest running creator-owned
book.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.909
We're up to issue 335.

00:18:13.909 --> 00:18:16.271
That's over 30 years of doing books.

00:18:16.271 --> 00:18:20.260
And now I do a monthly book every week.

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:21.260
You just get it done.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.460
It's just like going to the gym.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.120
Every workout isn't awesome.

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:26.820
But did you get your workout in?

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:27.820
Yeah, sure.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:29.000
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:30.300
By the hair of your chinny chin chin.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:31.440
Todd McFarlane: Nobody knows on the other
side.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.440
Nobody knows.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:33.440
Tim Ferriss: That's true, nobody knows.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:36.309
Todd McFarlane: Everybody's going to look
at that book and go, "Man, look how professionally

00:18:36.309 --> 00:18:38.260
done it was."

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:46.450
Tim Ferriss: So let's then come back to a
word you used, which is important, and that

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:51.440
is the longest running creator-owned superhero
comic book series, the creator-owned piece.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:52.780
Todd McFarlane: Yes.

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:57.110
Tim Ferriss: When and how did you decide to
start Image Comics?

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:01.380
Because I remember as a kid, I wanted to be
a penciller for about 10 years.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:02.380
Todd McFarlane: Oh, wow.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.770
Tim Ferriss: I was really tracking all of
this.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.910
And then I was an illustrator in high school
and then part of college.

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:13.480
I was the graphics editor in a magazine where
Jim Lee had been the previous graphics editor.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:14.480
So I had —
Todd McFarlane: Jim Lee.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.110
Yeah, Jim Lee, the head of DC —
Tim Ferriss: — the same desk that he'd use.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.750
Todd McFarlane: Jim Lee's the head of DC Comics,
for those who don't know.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:23.180
Tim Ferriss: And he had these sketches in
the desk that he'd done after getting hammered

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:24.679
in college.

00:19:24.679 --> 00:19:27.670
And I thought, these are just treasures at
the time.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:30.480
So this is a lot of fun for me to explore.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.299
And I remember Image being a very big deal.

00:19:32.299 --> 00:19:39.809
So for people who have no context, can you
explain sort of why and when Image was founded?

00:19:39.809 --> 00:19:43.240
Because I think that's a big piece of this
story.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.460
Todd McFarlane: Look, I assume that the majority
of people listening don't collect comic books

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:47.460
and whatever.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:48.460
So we'll keep it simple.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.660
Everybody knows Marvel and DC, everybody,
right?

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:54.720
If you ask the next natural question, "Huh?

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:56.640
Who's number three?"

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:59.370
That has been Image Comic Books for 30 years.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:03.350
We are celebrating our 30th anniversary with
the Spawn character, because it came out that

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.150
first year.

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:09.059
And we've been number three for 30 consecutive
years.

00:20:09.059 --> 00:20:12.560
As a matter of fact, those first couple months
we came out, we actually passed DC Comic Books.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:14.250
We were number two for a few months.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:21.360
So when people sort of get past the Marvel
and DC, and even in the industry of Hollywood,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:27.900
and or people that are looking for ancillary
products, which you have to because people

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:31.760
don't know Marvel's owned by Disney, right?

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:32.760
Marvel's owned by Disney.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.440
They don't share that too much.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:40.059
And DC Comic Books for a long, long time has
been owned by Time Warner, now Warner Bros.

00:20:40.059 --> 00:20:41.059
Discovery, AT&amp;T.

00:20:41.059 --> 00:20:45.140
But let's call it Warner Bros.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:48.710
So one's owned by Disney, one's owned by Warner
Bros.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:57.490
Okay, so your Sony, your Universal, your Paramount
Studios, your Netflix, your Apple, your Paramount

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:58.490
Plus, what are you talking about?

00:20:58.490 --> 00:20:59.520
Where you getting product?

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.700
Not getting it from Marvel.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:01.700
Why?

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:02.700
They're not sharing.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.280
And you're not getting it from DC because
they're not sharing.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:10.890
So you literally have to go and redact all
of those books.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:12.830
And you're left with everybody else.

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:17.159
And that puts our books in really good position.

00:21:17.159 --> 00:21:19.400
Now let's go back, because that doesn't answer
your question.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.420
How did we get to Image Comic Books?

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:27.059
For me personally, when I was trying to break
into comic books, like I said, in all those

00:21:27.059 --> 00:21:33.539
years leading up to breaking in, I was reading
everything I could get my hands on about our

00:21:33.539 --> 00:21:34.850
industry.

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:41.510
And what I found was I was coming across a
common theme.

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:51.250
And the common theme was that everybody, no
matter how big your standing had been in our

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:58.400
industry, eventually got pushed out against
their will, and in some cases got the short

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:02.080
end of creative and financial sticks.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.779
So I remember —
Tim Ferriss: These are artists?

00:22:03.779 --> 00:22:05.090
Todd McFarlane: These are artists, writers
—

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.090
Tim Ferriss: These are artists.

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:07.090
Todd McFarlane: — whatever.

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:09.909
These stories had been written over and over
and over again.

00:22:09.909 --> 00:22:13.490
And so I remember one in particular reading
about.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:19.270
There was a gentleman, his name was Jack Kirby,
and his nickname was Jack "The King" Kirby.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:25.500
To put in perspective to people who are layman
listening, that they called him The King for

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:30.240
a reason because he was, and he got the short
end of the stick.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:34.960
And Jack Kirby's the guy who helped create
The Fantastic Four and The X-Men and even

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:41.530
created the costume for Spider-Man and The
Hulk and Iron Man and on and on.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:43.929
That's who Jack Kirby was, right?

00:22:43.929 --> 00:22:46.760
He helped create it with Stan Lee, right?

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:52.650
So I remember reading those articles, and
this is long before I break into the industry,

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:59.710
and I went, "Man, if they can do that to Jack
"King" Kirby, they can do it to anyone."

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:06.640
And so when I got my first job in comic books,
three weeks before I graduated, I went in

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:09.049
with my eyes wide open.

00:23:09.049 --> 00:23:17.740
And so I knew what the game was, and I go,
"Okay, their job is to exploit me as much

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:19.780
as possible.

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:25.760
Can I do the same in reverse at the same time?"

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.250
The win is they're getting something of value
out of you, you're getting something out of

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.620
value out of them.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:36.130
And the value that I was getting out of them
was twofold.

00:23:36.130 --> 00:23:42.950
One, I had all these dozens of characters
in my portfolio, including Spawn, and I never

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:47.990
ever had one second temptation to ever pull
any of those out and offer them to Marvel

00:23:47.990 --> 00:23:49.760
or DC when I was working with them.

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:53.370
Did I create new characters when I got the
plots from the writers?

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:54.370
Of course I did.

00:23:54.370 --> 00:23:55.370
I was a professional.

00:23:55.370 --> 00:23:59.030
So I helped co-create and I'm the visual creator
of Venom, right?

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:00.490
So Venom's my guy.

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:01.490
Why?

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:03.550
Because we came up with a story and that was
what it was.

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:04.550
Okay, fine.

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:07.559
But I never said, "Oh, man, I'm having a good
career at Marvel.

00:24:07.559 --> 00:24:10.380
Let me reach into my portfolio and offer them
my characters."

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:11.590
Never.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:12.789
Why?

00:24:12.789 --> 00:24:18.100
Because in the back of my mind, those stories
had been haunting me that were there.

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:26.530
And at some point, Tim, I was selling more
comic books than any human being Marvel was

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:31.540
employing, period, to the point that I had
set a record on one of the books.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:34.730
I helped take over, artistically, Amazing
Spider-Man.

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:37.679
It was sitting at number 22 in the sales chart.

00:24:37.679 --> 00:24:41.240
They came, and they went, "Hey, Todd, if you
want to draw it, cool."

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:45.000
Because I had just finished a run on The Incredible
Hulk that you had mentioned.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.809
And they said, "Whatever you want to do, because
it's sort of in the dumper.

00:24:47.809 --> 00:24:48.809
Spider-Man's in the dumper."

00:24:48.809 --> 00:24:54.809
In short order, at some point, Amazing Spider-Man
became number one or two every single month

00:24:54.809 --> 00:24:58.540
in total sales, to the point then that they
were like, "Oh."

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:01.710
And it's really what catapulted my career.

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:06.309
And just so you know, all the things that
I was doing artistically to help move it from

00:25:06.309 --> 00:25:13.059
number 22 to number one, I was getting pushback
from the corporate entity up above and the

00:25:13.059 --> 00:25:18.429
executives up above and the editor in chief
up above that, "You can't do what you're doing,

00:25:18.429 --> 00:25:19.429
Todd.

00:25:19.429 --> 00:25:20.429
You're messing with the icon.

00:25:20.429 --> 00:25:21.429
That's not how we do it."

00:25:21.429 --> 00:25:26.779
Let me tell you, ladies and gentlemen, as
an old man now, the single greatest danger

00:25:26.779 --> 00:25:30.330
you are going to meet in your life is status
quo.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:36.710
It is the thing that they are going to fight
and battle you against more than any other

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:37.960
thing in the world.

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:43.730
And what's staggering about what I just said,
which is a truism, is that there's only two,

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:47.600
there's only two hundred-percenters in the
world that I can give you.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:49.460
One, we're all going to die.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:50.460
Hate to break it to you.

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:51.990
It's just a matter of time.

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:59.169
The second is everything is going to eventually
change, otherwise we'd be living in caves

00:25:59.169 --> 00:26:00.169
right now.

00:26:00.169 --> 00:26:03.850
Change is part of the human condition.

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:12.020
And yet every day you run into systems that
are crushingly holding on to status quo, are

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.840
holding on to yesterday.

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:22.360
And for those of us that are wired to think
about tomorrow, we become the rebels, we become

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:27.630
the outcasts, we become the people who are
rocking the boat, who are just, "Todd, why

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:30.620
don't you just relax, get along?"

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:32.299
All the things that they're going to tell
you.

00:26:32.299 --> 00:26:36.220
And what I'm saying that happened to me has
happened to millions of people throughout

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:39.779
history that want to do something different.

00:26:39.779 --> 00:26:40.779
Not better.

00:26:40.779 --> 00:26:44.179
Let me be very clear, not better, different.

00:26:44.179 --> 00:26:49.770
So when I was doing my different Spider-Man,
I wasn't doing it because I thought I was

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:50.770
better.

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:55.270
I wasn't doing it because I thought what they
were doing was worse, which is how they took

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:56.270
it.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:27:02.500
I was doing it because I'm a young kid with
a career and I need to figure out how to stand

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:06.480
out in a sea of people that are doing the
exact same job as me.

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:07.590
And there's only one way.

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.559
I've got to be a little bit different.

00:27:09.559 --> 00:27:15.380
So I started doing some funky fun little stuff
on Spider-Man.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.380
And guess what?

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:17.870
The fans liked it.

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:24.299
And more importantly, it was enjoyable for
me to draw because drawing is a lonely occupation,

00:27:24.299 --> 00:27:29.220
like a novelist, where you sit in a room for
12 hours a day with you and your thoughts,

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:30.730
that's your day.

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:34.400
And if you can't entertain yourself, it's
a long day.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:37.850
So I was coming up with crazy little silly
things I was putting in the book.

00:27:37.850 --> 00:27:39.310
They were having a heart attack.

00:27:39.310 --> 00:27:40.900
I was getting called on the carpet.

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:44.820
As the sales are going up, I finally quit
Spider-Man.

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:47.470
They go, No, no, no, because you're selling
so many books.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.000
We'll give you a new Spider-Man book.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:53.029
They were going to do a fourth Spider-Man
book anyway, so they could have one every

00:27:53.029 --> 00:27:54.899
week of the month.

00:27:54.899 --> 00:27:55.899
So they gave it to me.

00:27:55.899 --> 00:27:57.280
I've never written before.

00:27:57.280 --> 00:27:58.380
I'm going, "You're going to give it to me?"

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:00.370
I've never written before, but I have got
to write.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:01.370
Which is why I quit.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:02.890
I go, "I want to write."

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:06.059
And that's because I don't want to draw other
people's stories.

00:28:06.059 --> 00:28:08.190
I want to tell my story.

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:09.630
And they said, "Yeah, yeah, yeah."

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:10.909
And that book set a record.

00:28:10.909 --> 00:28:17.019
It's in the Guinness Book of World Records
for the most sales by a single creator.

00:28:17.019 --> 00:28:19.539
I also own that record on the other side with
Spawn.

00:28:19.539 --> 00:28:23.590
I own the corporate and non-corporate record
for a single issue by a single person.

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:24.590
Done.

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:29.640
Now I'm not saying that bragging, I'm just
saying that as a fact, so that now that guy

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:35.250
who's setting records when he comes into the
office in New York from Canada, little Canadian

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:41.250
hick, you would think that they would say,
"Thank you," not "Fuck you."

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:42.250
Right?

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:46.529
And instead they're calling me on the carpet
going, "Todd, you've got to stop doing this

00:28:46.529 --> 00:28:48.140
and this and this."

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:52.110
All the things that got me and their books
were there.

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:58.730
And I remember having these bizarre conversations
with the editors going, "Dude, you don't have

00:28:58.730 --> 00:29:03.480
to like me personally, you don't have to like
my drawing style, you don't have to like anything

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:04.480
I do.

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:08.110
You hired me for one job: sell comic books.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.059
You guys are in the commerce business.

00:29:10.059 --> 00:29:11.130
Don't you realize?

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:12.490
You're a money-making machine.

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:13.890
You want to sell books.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:17.539
I do that better than anybody that you employ.

00:29:17.539 --> 00:29:22.140
Why are we continuing to have these conversations?"

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:23.140
And they wore me out, Tim.

00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:24.140
They wore me out.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:28.350
Tim Ferriss: So, was there a particular moment
when you knew —

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:29.350
Todd McFarlane: Yes.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:32.789
Tim Ferriss: It seems like you had basically
in your back pocket the plan to eventually

00:29:32.789 --> 00:29:34.870
go out on your own, because you had all these
characters you developed.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:42.470
So what was the day or the conversation where
you're just like, fuck this, now is the time

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:43.470
I'm splitting off.

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:45.790
Todd McFarlane: I remember, with clarity.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:48.039
Comic books have this thing in a corner called
the Comics Code.

00:29:48.039 --> 00:29:52.620
And the Comics Code was created because in
the late '50s there was the whole Wertham

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:57.669
scare that comic books were degrading the
youth of America.

00:29:57.669 --> 00:29:59.640
And they had the Senate hearings.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:05.110
And it actually, in a weird way, ended up
leading to the advent of Marvel Comics.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:08.130
Because Marvel was a company called Timely
Comics.

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:12.660
But then they went, "Uh-oh, we don't want
to get caught up in this whole Senate hearing.

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:17.559
Let's whitewash, if you will, our presentation."

00:30:17.559 --> 00:30:21.720
And they changed their name to Marvel and
their first book out, Fantastic Four.

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:25.530
And then after that, here comes Spider-Man,
Iron Man, Hulk, and all the other things that

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:27.140
we all know.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:33.409
So you can argue that, thanks to some loony
tune in the Senate in the late '50s, Marvel

00:30:33.409 --> 00:30:34.630
exists.

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:36.309
Maybe minus him, there is no Marvel.

00:30:36.309 --> 00:30:40.490
So we actually shouldn't be giving all the
credit to Stan Lee, we should be giving it

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:41.490
to McCarthy.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:46.179
And Dr. Wertham, who was the one who wrote
the book, Seduction of the Innocent.

00:30:46.179 --> 00:30:47.490
So look it up.

00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:50.951
Anyways, I'm doing the books, because of that
Comics Code, every now and then they would

00:30:50.951 --> 00:30:52.520
get you to fix a panel.

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:53.919
"Todd, you can't do that."

00:30:53.919 --> 00:30:54.919
"Why?"

00:30:54.919 --> 00:30:55.919
"Because the Comics Code."

00:30:55.919 --> 00:30:59.919
"Okay," now I used to ask them, "could somebody
actually send me the Comics Code?

00:30:59.919 --> 00:31:04.730
So instead of having to guess what's in the
Comics Code, and then you guys tell me I've

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:09.769
got to redraw something, because I have deadlines
and I don't like to redraw and redo anything."

00:31:09.769 --> 00:31:13.200
And so it's like burning your pasta.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.210
Then you go, "Ah, man, I've got to cook it
and boil the water again."

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:18.270
You get aggravated the second time around.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:23.100
So at some point they kept doing that and
then the day came and then it was the proverbial

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:28.460
straw that broke the camel's back, because
I'd been doing it for years.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:34.770
And they sat there and it was this issue and
it was the Sideways issue, my last issue at

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.350
Marvel.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:38.470
It was a Sideways issue of the Spider-Man
book.

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:44.179
The one I was writing myself, except for,
we were doing a crossover with some of the

00:31:44.179 --> 00:31:50.350
mutant X-Men in this case, X-Factor characters,
the ones where Deadpool and Cable came from,

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:51.350
those characters.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:54.580
And there was a bad guy and his name was The
Juggernaut.

00:31:54.580 --> 00:31:57.169
And The Juggernaut was like, you couldn't
beat The Juggernaut, right?

00:31:57.169 --> 00:32:01.660
He's a big, giant dude and he had armor and
you couldn't do it.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:06.429
So my thing was, "Well he's got eye slits,
he's got to look out those eye slits."

00:32:06.429 --> 00:32:11.020
And so the way that I was going to get to
him was I had one of the mutants take their

00:32:11.020 --> 00:32:16.950
sword and put it in the eye and he'd jam it
in the eye because then it would be like,

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:21.950
oh, my gosh, you'd catch him off guard and
then if you team tackle him, you're going

00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:24.160
to win the day.

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:29.490
So I drew that, I still have that page today,
Tim, because it's the page that broke the

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:30.519
camel's back.

00:32:30.519 --> 00:32:34.299
I have the unedited version, even better.

00:32:34.299 --> 00:32:37.639
I don't even have the edited version, I have
the unedited versions.

00:32:37.639 --> 00:32:40.860
And so they phoned me up and they said, "Todd,
you've got to redraw it."

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:42.950
And I went, "What?

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:43.950
Redraw what?"

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:46.141
"You can't stab somebody in the eye with a
knife."

00:32:46.141 --> 00:32:48.130
And I go, "What are you talking about?"

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:49.130
"The Comics Code."

00:32:49.130 --> 00:32:56.370
And I go, "Well, of course you can, because
just not long ago there was this great cover,

00:32:56.370 --> 00:33:00.470
not only was it in, it was on the cover of
Daredevil, Frank Miller.

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:10.210
And he's got Bullseye on the cover and he's
stabbing Elektra, a character, on the cover."

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:12.590
And so I go, "Of course you can stab people."

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:19.630
And they go, "Well, yes, you can stab people,
but you can't have a rear exit wound."

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.230
And I went, "What are you talking about?

00:33:21.230 --> 00:33:22.590
Did you look at that cover?

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:23.799
The guy's killing Elektra.

00:33:23.799 --> 00:33:25.410
It's going in the front, it's coming out the
back."

00:33:25.410 --> 00:33:27.590
"No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:32.760
You can stab them and it can come out of an
exit wound, but you can't tear the cloth.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:36.370
So if you look at the drawing, you're going
to see that it's like, you don't really see

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:37.720
the sword coming out.

00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:38.940
It's like a teepee.

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:44.300
It's like a teepee, the back of her costume's
like a teepee, but it hasn't cut."

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:49.399
So I go, "So I just want to be clear, you
can gut somebody and you can have it come

00:33:49.399 --> 00:33:52.020
out the other side after you gut them.

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:56.370
You just can't rip the cloth.

00:33:56.370 --> 00:34:00.289
And that won't offend the mothers and the
children?"

00:34:00.289 --> 00:34:01.289
"Yes."

00:34:01.289 --> 00:34:02.289
"Wow.

00:34:02.289 --> 00:34:03.289
Okay.

00:34:03.289 --> 00:34:04.289
So there's no rear exit wound.

00:34:04.289 --> 00:34:05.720
I'm just stabbing them in the eye."

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:07.570
"Yeah, but you know what?

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:11.810
People are sensitive to eyes, because if everybody
gets something in their eye, we all know how

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:13.879
much pain that is and teeth.

00:34:13.879 --> 00:34:18.919
Because if you go to the dentist and he drills
you wrong…" and I'm having this absurd conversation

00:34:18.919 --> 00:34:24.929
with five or six of my editors and I'm just
going, "So I just want to be clear, can I

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:26.329
stab him in the chest?"

00:34:26.329 --> 00:34:27.329
"Yes."

00:34:27.329 --> 00:34:28.329
"In the knee?"

00:34:28.329 --> 00:34:29.329
"Yes."

00:34:29.329 --> 00:34:30.329
"In the elbow?"

00:34:30.329 --> 00:34:31.329
"Yes."

00:34:31.329 --> 00:34:32.329
"In the eye?"

00:34:32.329 --> 00:34:33.329
"No."

00:34:33.329 --> 00:34:34.329
"In the cheek?"

00:34:34.329 --> 00:34:35.329
"Yes."

00:34:35.329 --> 00:34:36.329
"In the neck?"

00:34:36.329 --> 00:34:37.329
"Yes."

00:34:37.329 --> 00:34:38.329
"In the mouth?"

00:34:38.329 --> 00:34:39.329
"No."

00:34:39.329 --> 00:34:40.329
"Wow."

00:34:40.329 --> 00:34:41.889
So somehow, I was in bizarre land.

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:46.859
And so at that moment I stopped the conversation,
because you never want to have a conversation

00:34:46.859 --> 00:34:51.269
with anybody where two plus two equals giraffe,
right?

00:34:51.269 --> 00:34:53.770
Never talk to somebody who comes out with
that equation.

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:57.050
So I said, "Guys, here's what's going to happen.

00:34:57.050 --> 00:34:59.500
I'm going to send you the page.

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:02.750
I'll do a little quick drawing over if you
want.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:08.060
You guys fix it any way you see fit.

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:10.660
By the way, I'm handing in my resignation.

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:11.720
I am done.

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:13.400
I am exhausted."

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:19.700
I also was a few days or away from becoming
a father for the first time.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:21.570
And I just went, you know what?

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:23.290
It's time to catch my breath.

00:35:23.290 --> 00:35:25.760
I don't know what being a dad's about.

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:27.560
These guys have worn me out.

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:30.660
And all I do is sell books for them.

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:33.480
And I just called uncle.

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:34.480
And that was it.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:36.020
That was the day I walked away.

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:37.820
Now, did I have a plan B?

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:39.230
Of course I did, Tim.

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:40.230
Of course I did.

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:46.230
The plan B was I had Spawn as a character
and I just went, I guess I'll just have to

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:47.790
self-publish, right?

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:51.820
Not ideal, but okay, here we go.

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:58.130
The upside of it was that I was talking to
a couple other people, let me also say before

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:02.880
me quitting, I also was going around trying
to create a union.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:04.300
I was like the Norma Rae.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:05.869
I'd just go, "Come on man.

00:36:05.869 --> 00:36:07.290
Power to the people.

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:11.340
If we stopped drawing, put pencils down, they've
got nothing to print."

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:13.810
We showed them our power.

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:20.349
It was frustrating for me when I tried it
for a few months that the most scared people

00:36:20.349 --> 00:36:27.040
that will say, "Yeah, let's join together,"
are those that don't have a job.

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:28.630
It was weird to me.

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:33.380
I'd be talking to dozens of people that I
knew that wanted to be in the business, that

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:36.730
were in the business, but weren't getting
steady work, that needed a better life.

00:36:36.730 --> 00:36:37.920
And I go, "Come on, let's just go.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:42.580
Let's just create an enclave of people and
again, deal with the whole is better than

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:43.580
the parts."

00:36:43.580 --> 00:36:47.180
And they would say, "No, Todd, I can't, because
what if they blackball me?"

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:49.119
And I'm like, "Blackball you?

00:36:49.119 --> 00:36:50.119
What are you talking about?

00:36:50.119 --> 00:36:51.770
You're not getting any work from them right
now."

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:53.580
"Yeah, but what if they blackball me?"

00:36:53.580 --> 00:36:57.020
I go, so I go, "Here, dude, here's what I'll
do for you.

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:03.660
Right now you have no job, no car, no girlfriend,
no house, no money.

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:08.810
If you join this enclave, I will promise I
will make all of those equal to you.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:13.160
You've got nothing to lose other than to go
up.

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:15.410
Come on, man.

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:17.340
I'm selling more books than anybody else.

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:22.380
I'm making more money than anybody else in
our industry and I'm willing to throw it away.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:25.490
You should be 10 times more fearless than
me."

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.760
It was actually oddly the opposite.

00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:34.600
So during that time, I had been talking to
two of my friends and peers.

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:38.839
They were also in the industry, a gentleman
by the name of Rob Liefeld and another one

00:37:38.839 --> 00:37:46.619
named Erik Larsen and both of them had that
same entrepreneurial, let's just call it rebellious

00:37:46.619 --> 00:37:48.060
trait in them.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:52.160
And we were always talking, and I remember
in one conversation Rob was saying, "Yeah,

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:56.510
I'm going to maybe go," he's still working
for Marvel, so was Erik.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:58.150
And it's like, "Yeah, I'm going to go do my
own book."

00:37:58.150 --> 00:38:01.680
And then Erik was on the call going, "Yeah,
I'm going to go do my own book."

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:03.560
And I had just quit.

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.280
And at some point during the conversation,
the topic came up and I go, "Well, if you're

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.880
going to do your own book on your own, Rob,
and you're going to do your own book on your

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:16.869
own, Erik, and I'm planning on it, why don't
we do all of them together in the same place?

00:38:16.869 --> 00:38:18.060
It's never been done."

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:23.829
What's happened is people have left Marvel
and DC one at a time, or they push them out

00:38:23.829 --> 00:38:24.829
the door.

00:38:24.829 --> 00:38:26.410
Think of it like a sports team.

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:29.300
So you lose a free agent, you'll go get another
player.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:30.300
So you lose another.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:36.530
But what if 10 of your players on a championship
team quit the same year?

00:38:36.530 --> 00:38:40.950
That would be detrimental to that competition
of that team.

00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:43.910
So the conversation was, "Why don't we just
join forces?"

00:38:43.910 --> 00:38:46.430
So all of a sudden, very quickly, it was three.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:51.730
Rob, who's super energetic, came up with the
name Image Comic Books.

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:56.520
And the reason why he came up with the name,
so he tells me, is that there was a commercial

00:38:56.520 --> 00:39:03.210
that was on TV and it was Andre Agassi, I
think, and it was a camera commercial.

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:06.270
And he says, "Image is everything."

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:09.410
So that was the punchline of the commercial.

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:11.250
So he was like, "Let's do Image.

00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:13.900
If image is everything, let's do Image."

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:16.270
So that was how Image Comic Books was born.

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:17.560
You don't overthink.

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:21.320
People think that we come up with all this
stuff and we know what we're doing.

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:22.320
No.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:24.230
Venom in and of itself was a happy accident.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:26.520
We can go back to that in a minute if you
want to.

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:34.109
But so the three of us get together and then
Rob says, "Todd, I've got a buddy, Jim Valentino,

00:39:34.109 --> 00:39:36.359
he does some independent comic books.

00:39:36.359 --> 00:39:37.450
Is it okay if he comes?"

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:40.110
And so I'm like, "What are you talking about?

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:41.520
This is a group.

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:42.520
The more the merrier."

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:50.470
So we've got four and then we find somebody
to help us publish and we say, that's it.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:55.760
We're flying to New York and we're going to
break the news to Marvel that we're quitting.

00:39:55.760 --> 00:40:02.069
Four of us, Rob, Erik, Todd, Jim Valentino.

00:40:02.069 --> 00:40:05.829
We fly to New York and I land the day before.

00:40:05.829 --> 00:40:11.480
We have a meeting with the top people, Terry
Stewart, who was the publishing head at that

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:12.880
point.

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:17.970
And later at that meeting I'll get to, was
the editor in chief who just happened to be

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.000
walking down the hall.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:25.460
So I got a meeting with Terry Stewart, the
top dog, and to basically say, "We're quitting.

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:27.250
Here's our reasons why.

00:40:27.250 --> 00:40:32.310
And if it was me, I would close this barn
door because you might have some more people

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:35.130
quitting next week or the week after, the
week after."

00:40:35.130 --> 00:40:39.630
So we land the day before and I've got to
blow some time.

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:40.830
So I happen to walk around.

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:44.130
I heard there's an auction, somebody's selling
some artwork.

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:48.930
I go to the auction and Jim Lee, the person
you know, you talked about at the beginning.

00:40:48.930 --> 00:40:49.930
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:51.340
Todd McFarlane: Who's now the head of DC Comic
Books.

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:56.490
Jim Lee is at the auction and he's like, "Hey,
Todd, what are you doing here?"

00:40:56.490 --> 00:41:01.060
I'm like, "Oh," And I told him, and then I
start giving the sales pitch.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:04.660
Tim, let me tell you one thing.

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:12.319
When I have my passion involved, I'm a good
salesman.

00:41:12.319 --> 00:41:14.390
I'm a good salesman.

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:18.270
And so I start pounding on Jim Lee.

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:21.720
And Jim Lee at this point, just so you know,
is doing The X-Men.

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:26.710
And it is the number-one-selling book, he's
doing the number-one-selling book.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:28.680
The only time I ever got beat was Jim Lee.

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:29.680
That guy.

00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:31.480
He was my competition.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:35.480
So I was Magic Johnson, he was Larry Bird.

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:39.540
And so we had our rival, but we liked each
other and we got along.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:44.870
So I tell him what we're doing and he starts
thinking about it.

00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:51.329
And then to my surprise says, "I think I can
go with that."

00:41:51.329 --> 00:41:58.360
Now, this is a monumental moment from my perspective,
and here's why, ladies and gentlemen.

00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:04.800
Todd the rebel leaving was going to be easy
for Marvel to basically discount, because

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:09.550
they were going to go, "That kid's always
rocking the boat and he's always a bit of

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:10.550
a pistol.

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:11.550
You know what?

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:12.550
That's fine."

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:13.920
Rob Liefeld had the same attitude.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:16.040
So it's like, we were the bad boys.

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:17.610
"So the bad boys left.

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:18.970
Good riddance."

00:42:18.970 --> 00:42:22.030
Jim Lee was the golden child.

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.829
He was the chef's kiss.

00:42:24.829 --> 00:42:26.250
He was the guy.

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:28.130
He was perfect.

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:34.150
If they could clone employees and artists,
Jim Lee was the mold.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:41.680
And so when Jim said he would go, that was
a thunderclap in my head to go, "Oh, my God.

00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:46.849
If the choir boy can go, then that means all
bets are off.

00:42:46.849 --> 00:42:51.470
And they're going to have to sit up and pay
attention, because not only are you losing

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:54.599
the bad boys, you are losing the model citizens."

00:42:54.599 --> 00:42:59.630
And so Jim then says, "Oh, by the way, I got
a pal, Whilce Portacio.

00:42:59.630 --> 00:43:02.339
Is it okay if I phone him?

00:43:02.339 --> 00:43:05.050
Because he's looking for work, I think he'll
join too."

00:43:05.050 --> 00:43:08.250
Shoot, he was doing another X-Men book.

00:43:08.250 --> 00:43:09.410
I'm going, what?

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:10.500
We're going to get two X-Men?

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:13.410
The X-Men books are the number one selling
book at that point.

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:14.900
Bring them on.

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:19.640
So we got six and I'm walking now back to
my hotel with the biggest grin on my face

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:22.300
because I go, "They don't even know it's about
to come."

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:29.309
And as I'm walking into my hotel, I see another
peer, a guy by the name of Marc Silvestri.

00:43:29.309 --> 00:43:34.720
Now Marc Silvestri to me, at that time, was
the best artist.

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:37.890
Just in terms of skill, Marc Silvestri.

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:40.510
Bob McFarlane: Don't believe a thing that
my son is saying.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:42.680
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, that's my dad, I'm taking
care of him.

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:43.859
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, no problem.

00:43:43.859 --> 00:43:44.859
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

00:43:44.859 --> 00:43:48.130
So Marc Silvestri's there and it's about 11:00
o'clock at night and I go, "Hey Marc, what

00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:49.130
are you doing?"

00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:50.130
He goes, "Oh, I'm going to bed."

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:51.990
"You got five minutes for me?"

00:43:51.990 --> 00:43:58.020
And so I sit down and like Mussolini from
the balcony, I give him this speech and I

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:00.460
give it to him and he's like, "Oh, my God."

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:02.890
He goes, "Todd, that sounds good.

00:44:02.890 --> 00:44:03.890
And Jim Lee's on board?"

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:05.380
"Yeah, he just signed on."

00:44:05.380 --> 00:44:06.750
"Let me think about it."

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:08.950
I go, "Marc, here's the gig.

00:44:08.950 --> 00:44:13.730
We've got a meeting at 8:00 o'clock tomorrow
morning; I need to know if you're in or not

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:14.760
by 8:00 o'clock in the morning."

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.040
He goes, "So I've got to go to bed, I've got
to think about it whether I'm going to change

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:23.930
my entire career and I've got nine hours,
of which eight of them I'm going to sleep."

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:24.960
"Yeah, that's it."

00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:29.560
Now, I don't know what he did or how well
he slept that night, but in the morning at

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:32.160
7:30 the phone rings and he goes, "Hey Todd,
Marc.

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.579
I'm in."

00:44:33.579 --> 00:44:34.720
Seven.

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:40.059
We went to New York, I flew to New York with
Rob, with four of us, with four of us.

00:44:40.059 --> 00:44:42.790
And by the time we walk into that office,
we've got seven.

00:44:42.790 --> 00:44:46.400
Oh, by the way, Marc Silvestri was doing the
Wolverine comic book.

00:44:46.400 --> 00:44:52.770
We had literally the dream team, to put in
perspective for people listening again that

00:44:52.770 --> 00:44:59.640
don't know, there's probably, every year about,
that year was probably about six, 7,000 comic

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:04.890
books come out from all companies, because
again, Spider-Man comes out once a month,

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:05.890
so it's 12.

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:09.430
And then you've got three Spider-Man books,
that's 36 and then Iron Man's another 12 and

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:10.430
12.

00:45:10.430 --> 00:45:11.430
And you add it all up.

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:13.820
Literally thousands of comic books come out.

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:25.569
The people who gathered together to create
Image, we had accomplished 44 of the top 50

00:45:25.569 --> 00:45:27.000
sales that year.

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:33.470
Just to put in perspective who we were, 44
of the top 50 sales of 6,000.

00:45:33.470 --> 00:45:39.530
We were literally the comic book equivalent
of the Dream Team, the basketball team that

00:45:39.530 --> 00:45:41.800
was happening in basketball at that time.

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:45.100
So I knew this was going to reverberate somewhere.

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:49.470
And again, the stock went down at Marvel the
next day, take a look at it, because CNN reported

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:51.079
on it.

00:45:51.079 --> 00:45:54.530
And we went into the meeting and essentially
it went like this.

00:45:54.530 --> 00:46:00.030
And here would be a fun interview, is to get
Terry Stewart's perspective of it.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:07.540
Because not only has he literally lied about
it, he will tell you otherwise, but not only

00:46:07.540 --> 00:46:11.710
has he lied about it, but then 30 years later,
I met up with him and my wife went and talked

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:17.089
to him and he still is spewing the lie.

00:46:17.089 --> 00:46:18.089
And I get it.

00:46:18.089 --> 00:46:19.089
He's corporate.

00:46:19.089 --> 00:46:20.089
What's he going to do?

00:46:20.089 --> 00:46:24.000
He doesn't want to say that it was on his
watch that all these people left.

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:26.069
But here's his lie.

00:46:26.069 --> 00:46:30.040
And it is, because I've got witnesses, because
there's more than one of us in that room.

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:35.960
Is, he's saying, we came in and we asked for
the copyrights of our characters.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:37.070
What are you talking about?

00:46:37.070 --> 00:46:39.100
None of us is that crazy.

00:46:39.100 --> 00:46:41.829
We understood the dynamics of the business.

00:46:41.829 --> 00:46:45.300
"Yeah, give me Spider-Man and give him The
X-Men."

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:47.840
What are you talking about?

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:51.230
He's saying he had to let us go because that
was our demand.

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:52.740
No, no.

00:46:52.740 --> 00:46:58.339
The way that it went down was simply like
this, "We are leaving.

00:46:58.339 --> 00:47:01.910
There's nothing you can give us that's going
to keep us.

00:47:01.910 --> 00:47:05.260
And oh, by the way, here's some of our reasons.

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:11.869
If it was us, you may want to do something
about those reasons, because next week you

00:47:11.869 --> 00:47:14.260
may have another seven quitting.

00:47:14.260 --> 00:47:18.400
I don't understand why you want to keep having
people quitting, but you know what?

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:21.619
Do what you've got to do, It's your company,
whatever."

00:47:21.619 --> 00:47:26.930
Now during that conversation the editor in
chief, which coincidentally happened to be

00:47:26.930 --> 00:47:32.690
up on the upper floors, don't know why, and
just came in and whatever.

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:39.270
He was a good man, Tom DeFalco, one that used
to tell me, "Can't do that, Todd.

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:41.200
You can't do that, Todd, on Spider-Man."

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:46.230
But so anyway, at the end of this conversation,
because Rob Liefeld, who was in there and

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:52.330
my wife was there and Jim Lee was there, Rob
left because he had to go pick up his girlfriend.

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:58.390
So that was classic Rob, like, "Oh, you guys
just finished this world-changing conversation.

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:01.119
I've got to go get my wife and got to grab
a burger."

00:48:01.119 --> 00:48:07.900
And this is why I like Rob so much, because
he's by the cuff and it's what makes him special

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:08.900
to me.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:14.859
Anyways, we get in the elevator at the end
of this conversation and I will never forget

00:48:14.859 --> 00:48:19.070
the words, as the elevator doors are closing,
just like in a movie.

00:48:19.070 --> 00:48:24.380
The doors are closing and you can see the
editor in chief looking at us and he says,

00:48:24.380 --> 00:48:27.990
"Hey, if it doesn't work, you're welcome back."

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:30.500
And the doors shut.

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:35.960
And I remember turning to Jim Lee, now, just
to give a little background on Jim Lee.

00:48:35.960 --> 00:48:38.819
Jim Lee went to college to become a doctor.

00:48:38.819 --> 00:48:40.450
I think his dad's a doctor.

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:43.780
This is a smart, intelligent human being.

00:48:43.780 --> 00:48:48.520
And for me, my dad, who you just heard was
in the printing business for 40, 50 years.

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:54.780
The one thing that I knew it's printing, and
if not, I knew lots of people who did.

00:48:54.780 --> 00:49:02.010
So the doors closed and I looked at Jim and
I go, "Oh, my God, they think we're dumb."Oh,

00:49:02.010 --> 00:49:03.770
and oh, not that we're smart, Tim.

00:49:03.770 --> 00:49:07.910
Not that we're smart, Tim, but you know what
printing comic books is?

00:49:07.910 --> 00:49:09.980
Ink on paper.

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:12.140
That's it, ladies and gentlemen.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:14.160
Ink on paper.

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:15.240
Done.

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:19.210
Maybe there's a couple details beyond that,
but that's it.

00:49:19.210 --> 00:49:23.750
Every time you pick up a pen and you write
on a piece of paper, it's like doing comic

00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:24.750
books.

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:27.910
It's just, if you keep going and draw it in
the shape of Spider-Man or the Hulk, you've

00:49:27.910 --> 00:49:29.329
got a comic book.

00:49:29.329 --> 00:49:31.540
It's not rocket science.

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:35.060
And so I was like, "Oh, my God, they don't
think we can print.

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:36.110
Oh, my God."

00:49:36.110 --> 00:49:38.369
So that was it.

00:49:38.369 --> 00:49:42.910
And from there, the collective whole of the
seven of us left.

00:49:42.910 --> 00:49:47.410
Here's this silly funny part of the rest of
that day.

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:52.920
Historically what was happening, Tim, is that
if you quit Marvel, you only had one choice.

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:59.250
You went literally across the street to DC
Comics, or if you quit DC Comics, you quit

00:49:59.250 --> 00:50:00.740
and you walked across the street to Marvel.

00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:01.740
That's all you did.

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:05.049
You literally ping ponged your whole career
back and forth, back and forth.

00:50:05.049 --> 00:50:08.960
Whenever you got mad, you just went to the
other date that was across the street.

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:13.869
So we went across the street to DC Comic Books
and DC sees, I had done about a year, year

00:50:13.869 --> 00:50:15.320
and a half there.

00:50:15.320 --> 00:50:18.060
Rob Liefeld had done a year there.

00:50:18.060 --> 00:50:19.510
But Jim Lee and I walk in there.

00:50:19.510 --> 00:50:23.470
Jim Lee has never stepped foot in DC Comic
Books.

00:50:23.470 --> 00:50:26.609
And he's the golden child at Marvel.

00:50:26.609 --> 00:50:30.270
And they just go, "There's only one reason
he's in the office.

00:50:30.270 --> 00:50:34.950
Somebody got mad and we're getting the golden
child and we're getting Todd?

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:39.190
We'll take the bad boy too, because these
are the number-one- and number-two-selling

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:40.450
guys in the industry.

00:50:40.450 --> 00:50:41.450
Woo.

00:50:41.450 --> 00:50:42.800
We hit the mother lode.

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:44.319
The mother lode, oh, my God."

00:50:44.319 --> 00:50:50.500
And quickly they assemble 15, 16 people in
this room and they pour the coffee and they

00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:54.210
get us the refreshments and they just go,
"Oh, my goodness."

00:50:54.210 --> 00:50:55.210
We are sitting here.

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:58.940
And we sit down and then they hear the fateful
words.

00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:03.460
I go, "Hey, guys, just so you know, we're
not here to work for you.

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:04.460
Just a couple things.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:05.460
We just quit Marvel."

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:06.460
Their eyes light up.

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:09.609
"But we are not here to work for you either."

00:51:09.609 --> 00:51:11.859
And you could just see the lead balloon.

00:51:11.859 --> 00:51:15.620
And then we're like, and it's like, "No, no,
no, we're not here to work for you."

00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:16.620
And you could just see, "So we're just clear
here.

00:51:16.620 --> 00:51:23.930
So you came here with Jim Lee and walked into
our offices and are now dangling and teasing

00:51:23.930 --> 00:51:27.099
us and saying it's for nothing?"

00:51:27.099 --> 00:51:30.490
"That's exactly what we're saying."

00:51:30.490 --> 00:51:31.490
"Wow.

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:32.490
Wow.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:33.490
Why would you guys do that?"

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:36.770
And I go, "Well, because we've got some other
plans."

00:51:36.770 --> 00:51:38.550
And then they start giving their sales pitch.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:41.000
"Well, here's why you should work for us.

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:42.000
Don't do that.

00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:43.220
DC is your place to be.

00:51:43.220 --> 00:51:44.960
DC, DC, DC."

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:49.000
And they go, "And by the way, we just did
a new contract that's for the betterment of

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:51.000
the creative community."

00:51:51.000 --> 00:51:55.430
And then I asked them the question for me
that was like the dagger for me, where I go,

00:51:55.430 --> 00:51:57.079
"Hey, no, that was super cool.

00:51:57.079 --> 00:51:58.290
That was super, super cool.

00:51:58.290 --> 00:52:00.990
You wrote up that new contract for the creative
community.

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:05.820
Could I just ask you just one question here
just before we get going?

00:52:05.820 --> 00:52:12.390
Did you ask one fucking creator to have any
input in that contract that is to better the

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:13.490
creative community?

00:52:13.490 --> 00:52:17.460
Did you ask one single creator?"

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:22.950
And let me tell you, Tim, when you get pregnant
pauses in rooms, it's all you need to know.

00:52:22.950 --> 00:52:25.260
You get your answer at the pregnant pause.

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:27.130
Of course they didn't.

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:28.790
Of course they didn't.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:30.480
And that was the reason.

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:32.849
"This is why we're quitting Marvel.

00:52:32.849 --> 00:52:40.750
This is why we're quitting DC, because of
your disregard for your community."

00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:46.099
And Jim and I are examples, and we've climbed
the top of the ladder, just like Jack King

00:52:46.099 --> 00:52:49.080
Kirby and all those hundreds before him.

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:55.490
This is just a repeat of history, and it's
time.

00:52:55.490 --> 00:52:57.490
And we thought that that was it.

00:52:57.490 --> 00:53:03.030
And that was when the collective whole, not
me, the collective whole, the seven of us,

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:04.290
started Image Comics.

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:10.210
Tim Ferriss: So, the DC visit, I'm trying
to think of the names involved.

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:14.359
Was there anyone who was quitting DC or did
you just go in there to put them on alert

00:53:14.359 --> 00:53:15.359
and say —
Todd McFarlane: No, no.

00:53:15.359 --> 00:53:16.359
We came up —
Tim Ferriss: "Listen up."

00:53:16.359 --> 00:53:18.710
Todd McFarlane: We put them on alert and gave
them the same sort of speech we gave Marvel,

00:53:18.710 --> 00:53:23.490
"If you think that the dissatisfaction of
the seven of us is unique to the seven of

00:53:23.490 --> 00:53:25.109
us, you guys are blind.

00:53:25.109 --> 00:53:26.109
It is…"

00:53:26.109 --> 00:53:27.109
Tim Ferriss: I see.

00:53:27.109 --> 00:53:30.150
It was a, "Change your ways or become a fatality."

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:31.380
Todd McFarlane: Right.

00:53:31.380 --> 00:53:36.470
Every conversation is the exact same and you're
just going to — and if we prove that there's

00:53:36.470 --> 00:53:43.800
any success on the outside of the only two
bubbles that exist in most freelancers' sort

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:47.940
of brains, which is Marvel, DC, or you get
another job.

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:50.960
And I mean another job in another industry.

00:53:50.960 --> 00:53:58.430
So, if we move on and we create a third possibility,
why do that?

00:53:58.430 --> 00:54:05.250
Now, to their credit, let me say, Tim, to
their credit, they did start changing.

00:54:05.250 --> 00:54:11.869
They did start bettering pay and even starting
to give medical, which was never a part of

00:54:11.869 --> 00:54:18.170
the equation, and giving bonuses and being
a little more fair-minded on royalties because

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:25.300
they knew that what we were saying at some
point, that reality sunk in, that we could

00:54:25.300 --> 00:54:32.299
start losing almost all of our top talent,
if not a big portion of our talent.

00:54:32.299 --> 00:54:34.420
And this isn't good for business.

00:54:34.420 --> 00:54:42.140
So even those that were jealous in our creative
community and/or thought that we were crazies

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:47.310
or whatever, they still, whether they know
it or not, prospered by us leaving because

00:54:47.310 --> 00:54:51.890
all their contracts got upgraded while they
were still throwing darts at us.

00:54:51.890 --> 00:54:57.740
Our own community, as you can imagine, saying,
"You guys go take your big egos and go do

00:54:57.740 --> 00:54:58.740
your thing."

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:01.900
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so I just came back from
a bathroom break.

00:55:01.900 --> 00:55:05.280
You mentioned before we started recording
you had a camel bladder and that you can talk

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:07.670
until I had to take a bathroom break, which
was true.

00:55:07.670 --> 00:55:11.430
So, how is a camel bladder a competitive advantage?

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:13.920
Todd McFarlane: It is.

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:17.099
Because people go, "So you don't go to the
bathroom?"

00:55:17.099 --> 00:55:22.450
I mean, I went to a signing not long ago and
I got there at seven and I signed from seven

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:27.230
in the morning to midnight and I didn't move
from the desk where I was signing.

00:55:27.230 --> 00:55:30.760
Again, other human beings had to eat and go
to the bathroom during.

00:55:30.760 --> 00:55:31.970
I go, "You'd better have two teams, then.

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:33.590
You're going to have to rotate them."

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:40.160
Let me just quickly get out of the way first
how you cannot go to the bathroom for 17 hours.

00:55:40.160 --> 00:55:42.390
It's really sort of easy stuff.

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:46.030
If you don't put anything into your top hole,
nothing comes out of any of the bottom holes.

00:55:46.030 --> 00:55:48.120
It's just basic.

00:55:48.120 --> 00:55:49.740
I don't eat and I don't drink.

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:52.020
That's a whole 'nother conversation.

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:54.309
But they're going, "How do you do that?"

00:55:54.309 --> 00:55:55.309
That's another conversation.

00:55:55.309 --> 00:55:58.170
But if you don't put anything, nothing in,
nothing out.

00:55:58.170 --> 00:55:59.430
Simple, easy.

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:03.280
And I know this to be scientifically true
because I've used it to my advantage.

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:08.870
Now, because I have a camel bladder, two things
have happened in my career that I think have

00:56:08.870 --> 00:56:10.100
been advantageous.

00:56:10.100 --> 00:56:13.880
One, everybody, when I go to the conventions,
would have to go and take a lunch break.

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:14.880
Why?

00:56:14.880 --> 00:56:15.880
Because they're hungry.

00:56:15.880 --> 00:56:17.010
They're hungry, right?

00:56:17.010 --> 00:56:20.030
My wife would tell you, I have never uttered
those words, "I'm hungry."

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:22.789
I eat because science says I need to.

00:56:22.789 --> 00:56:24.210
My body needs fuel.

00:56:24.210 --> 00:56:29.619
So I put it in, not because I'm hungry, because
I have to, it's an essential ingredient.

00:56:29.619 --> 00:56:30.680
Food.

00:56:30.680 --> 00:56:34.400
I do it, but I can outlast you if you've got
to go away.

00:56:34.400 --> 00:56:39.480
People would take off at conventions and go
away for lunch or bathroom breaks or whatever.

00:56:39.480 --> 00:56:40.789
Guess who didn't me?

00:56:40.789 --> 00:56:41.789
Me.

00:56:41.789 --> 00:56:43.380
And here's why that matters.

00:56:43.380 --> 00:56:46.089
Because there's people in line.

00:56:46.089 --> 00:56:50.550
At the time of our popularity, when they used
to open it up, you'd have at times, I'm telling

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:53.980
you, literally thousands of people in line
waiting for your autograph.

00:56:53.980 --> 00:56:59.119
First off, in good conscience, I can't have
somebody waiting in line for two hours, three

00:56:59.119 --> 00:57:01.359
hours and then look at them and say, "Cut.

00:57:01.359 --> 00:57:03.560
I'm going for an hour and a half lunch break."

00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:04.839
And the kid's going, "What?

00:57:04.839 --> 00:57:05.901
I've already been in line for three hours."

00:57:05.901 --> 00:57:06.940
I can't do it.

00:57:06.940 --> 00:57:09.130
I can't look at somebody in the eye, I won't
do it.

00:57:09.130 --> 00:57:16.070
I said, "No, I'll just figure out how to not
do this thing that most humans do."

00:57:16.070 --> 00:57:20.410
And here's where the upside is, is that when
all my peers have gone to lunch, then people

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:24.240
are waiting in line and then they're just
sitting there going, "Well, that guy's still

00:57:24.240 --> 00:57:25.240
signing.

00:57:25.240 --> 00:57:26.240
What's his name?

00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:27.450
Todd, Tom, Tim, whatever.

00:57:27.450 --> 00:57:28.650
What's he do?

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:29.650
Spider-Man?

00:57:29.650 --> 00:57:30.650
Well, I like comic books.

00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:31.650
I know Spider-Man."

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:33.060
And guess what happens, Tim?

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:34.250
They get in your line.

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:36.630
They don't care really at that point about
you.

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:38.250
They just go, "He's signing.

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:40.190
That line's moving, I'll get in his line."

00:57:40.190 --> 00:57:44.970
And then they come up, you've got 20 seconds,
you become as gracious as you can to them

00:57:44.970 --> 00:57:50.120
and maybe you peel off and you've got a new
couple fans and all of a sudden it's like,

00:57:50.120 --> 00:57:51.680
"I only collect X-Men.

00:57:51.680 --> 00:57:57.420
But that Todd was a gentleman and he was very
nice and he smiled at me, he was very kind.

00:57:57.420 --> 00:57:58.420
You know what?

00:57:58.420 --> 00:58:02.250
Maybe I'll go buy one of his Spider-Man books
or, in the future, his Spawn books."

00:58:02.250 --> 00:58:03.250
And so that's it.

00:58:03.250 --> 00:58:04.250
Good, right?

00:58:04.250 --> 00:58:07.190
I'll always be nice to anybody no matter who
they are.

00:58:07.190 --> 00:58:08.829
Number two on the business end.

00:58:08.829 --> 00:58:14.030
And this one is sadly even easier and it's
just pathetic at times.

00:58:14.030 --> 00:58:15.740
I live in Phoenix, Arizona.

00:58:15.740 --> 00:58:18.609
And Phoenix, Arizona can be 110 degrees.

00:58:18.609 --> 00:58:20.240
Let me tell you, I'm like a cockroach.

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:22.960
I don't care what the weather is, I'll survive.

00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:24.750
Don't worry about me, I'm good.

00:58:24.750 --> 00:58:26.450
But here's what I know about other people.

00:58:26.450 --> 00:58:27.940
They have comfort zones.

00:58:27.940 --> 00:58:32.900
And so whenever I was in some big legal dispute
or contract dispute, I would say, especially

00:58:32.900 --> 00:58:36.380
the people in L.A., I go, "You fly them to
me, you fly them to me."

00:58:36.380 --> 00:58:39.240
Now, this is just the art of war.

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:45.920
My desk is facing a big giant glass window,
and I know when the sun comes down that window.

00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:50.960
Now, usually when I'm in my room, I bring
down the drapes and I put up the AC and I'm

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:51.960
comfortable.

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:57.730
But when the enemy's coming, i.e., the people
I've got to negotiate with come, I make sure

00:58:57.730 --> 00:59:03.720
that the blinds are up and that I turn the
air conditioning so that it's actually stuffy

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:05.299
in that room because why?

00:59:05.299 --> 00:59:06.299
I can endure it.

00:59:06.299 --> 00:59:07.380
I've done it plenty of times.

00:59:07.380 --> 00:59:11.650
And then they always come dressed in a three-piece
suit.

00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:13.089
Wrong move, guys.

00:59:13.089 --> 00:59:14.089
Wrong move.

00:59:14.089 --> 00:59:17.559
And then they come in and they come into the
room and I close the door and I know it's

00:59:17.559 --> 00:59:23.880
going to get stuffy, and then I put a giant
pitcher of water in front of them, and then

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:30.120
I utter the words, "Guys, this has been going
on far, far too long.

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:31.780
Here's the deal.

00:59:31.780 --> 00:59:39.059
We're not leaving this room until we settle
every single outstanding point."

00:59:39.059 --> 00:59:46.140
And during those conversations, they either
pour their own water on a constant basis because

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.740
they've got their three-piece wool suit on.

00:59:48.740 --> 00:59:57.260
I've got my t-shirt and I'm in my Nike shorts
and I can just see them getting hotter and

00:59:57.260 --> 01:00:01.500
hotter and hotter, and they keep having to
get more and more water.

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:07.420
And at some point, nature calls and then we
get down to the last one and I go, "I'm not

01:00:07.420 --> 01:00:09.670
moving on point 10, I'm not moving.

01:00:09.670 --> 01:00:10.670
It's a deal breaker.

01:00:10.670 --> 01:00:12.079
If I don't get it, this whole deal..."

01:00:12.079 --> 01:00:16.260
And how many times I've had people in my room
go, "Fine."

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:18.540
This is how it works, it goes just, "Fine.

01:00:18.540 --> 01:00:19.760
You can have point 10.

01:00:19.760 --> 01:00:20.760
Are we done?"

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:21.760
"Yes."

01:00:21.760 --> 01:00:22.760
"Are we done?"

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:23.760
"Yes."

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:24.760
"Where's your bathroom?"

01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:26.150
The very next thing is, "Where's your bathroom?"

01:00:26.150 --> 01:00:34.460
Because I think in my mind that they literally
caved in on the 10th point because they couldn't

01:00:34.460 --> 01:00:36.319
hold it anymore.

01:00:36.319 --> 01:00:40.420
And if they could have held it, they could
have argued longer with me, and perhaps I

01:00:40.420 --> 01:00:46.200
might have conceded or we could have compromised
on that point, but their bladder costs them.

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:48.539
Too bad, how sad, right?

01:00:48.539 --> 01:00:51.549
You find your advantages wherever you can
get them.

01:00:51.549 --> 01:00:53.950
Tim Ferriss: Oh, McFarlane, the barbarian.

01:00:53.950 --> 01:00:55.900
What a savage.

01:00:55.900 --> 01:00:57.200
Nice work.

01:00:57.200 --> 01:00:59.010
So, let's come back to Venom.

01:00:59.010 --> 01:01:04.339
You mentioned that Venom came together, I
think, as an accident maybe was the phrasing

01:01:04.339 --> 01:01:05.339
that you used.

01:01:05.339 --> 01:01:06.339
I mean, this is an iconic character known
the world over.

01:01:06.339 --> 01:01:07.339
Todd McFarlane: A happy, happy accident.

01:01:07.339 --> 01:01:08.339
Happy accident.

01:01:08.339 --> 01:01:09.339
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:01:09.339 --> 01:01:10.339
So how did it come together?

01:01:10.339 --> 01:01:11.339
Todd McFarlane: We'll go back early in my
Marvel career.

01:01:11.339 --> 01:01:16.020
Like I said early in the podcast, if you can
keep deadlines, that's a giant value in that

01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:17.510
industry.

01:01:17.510 --> 01:01:21.900
I was showing them that I could keep deadlines,
so I knew then that was going to be getting

01:01:21.900 --> 01:01:23.309
me continuous work.

01:01:23.309 --> 01:01:31.369
Once you get continuous work, the next upgrade
is can you draw characters that people have

01:01:31.369 --> 01:01:32.740
heard of, right?

01:01:32.740 --> 01:01:37.869
The first job I got at Marvel was on a book
and it was an obscure book.

01:01:37.869 --> 01:01:40.660
It was called Steve Englehart's Coyote.

01:01:40.660 --> 01:01:41.849
And it wasn't even Coyote.

01:01:41.849 --> 01:01:44.079
I was doing a backup in the Coyote.

01:01:44.079 --> 01:01:46.970
I was doing an obscure backup in an obscure
book.

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:50.789
It literally was starting in the mail room.

01:01:50.789 --> 01:01:58.799
But eventually, I got a steady job over at
DC because they canceled Coyote, and I got

01:01:58.799 --> 01:02:00.200
a monthly book over at DC.

01:02:00.200 --> 01:02:07.430
Unfortunately, Tim, and this is where sometimes
one person's break is another person's tragedy.

01:02:07.430 --> 01:02:12.920
An artist on another book who was a fit human
being, was a super awesome artist, I followed

01:02:12.920 --> 01:02:19.760
him, was a health freak, drank some unpasteurized
milk, went into a seizure, and had an allergic

01:02:19.760 --> 01:02:20.760
reaction and died.

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:21.760
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God.

01:02:21.760 --> 01:02:26.789
Todd McFarlane: I get a phone call and they
go, "Hey, Todd, my artist just passed away,

01:02:26.789 --> 01:02:29.920
can you come on and help us for a couple months?"

01:02:29.920 --> 01:02:32.760
And at that point, they had canceled Coyote.

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:34.119
Literally took me years to get in.

01:02:34.119 --> 01:02:37.539
I was employed for four months and then they
canceled Coyote.

01:02:37.539 --> 01:02:39.200
And I'm like, "Man..."

01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:44.370
I sent my samples back to all the people that
were gracious enough to give me constructive

01:02:44.370 --> 01:02:50.280
criticism, except for a thing changed on the
resume, Tim, I was now able to say, "I am

01:02:50.280 --> 01:02:53.420
Todd McFarlane, the professional from Marvel
Comic Books."

01:02:53.420 --> 01:02:57.390
The drawing was exactly the same, it'd only
been four months, it was just as horrible

01:02:57.390 --> 01:02:59.340
as it was four months earlier.

01:02:59.340 --> 01:03:03.329
Except instead of being an amateur, I got
to go now into the smaller pile, which is

01:03:03.329 --> 01:03:06.650
"The professional is looking for work" pile.

01:03:06.650 --> 01:03:07.650
I get the job.

01:03:07.650 --> 01:03:09.480
I go, "Yeah, I've got some work."

01:03:09.480 --> 01:03:12.760
The guy who was supposed to take over that
book, because I was only supposed to fill

01:03:12.760 --> 01:03:16.620
in for two months, decided to bail.

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:19.670
After the first issue they said, "Do you want
to take over the book?"

01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:21.070
It was a book called Infinity, Inc.

01:03:21.070 --> 01:03:23.099
And I stayed on that book for two, three years.

01:03:23.099 --> 01:03:26.750
So, when I went over to Marvel, they went,
"Okay, you can keep a deadline.

01:03:26.750 --> 01:03:27.750
Check.

01:03:27.750 --> 01:03:31.070
The question is, you've got to stop doing..."

01:03:31.070 --> 01:03:35.090
And here's a bizarre thing that was happening
at Marvel at that point, "You've got to stop

01:03:35.090 --> 01:03:37.960
doing," what they called, they dubbed, "your
big dice drawing style."

01:03:37.960 --> 01:03:43.070
And the only reason it was called that because
on one page in Infinity Inc., I drew this

01:03:43.070 --> 01:03:45.579
page layout and it was these big giant dice.

01:03:45.579 --> 01:03:48.750
And then I did panels inside, drawings inside
the big dice.

01:03:48.750 --> 01:03:52.319
And somebody, I guess in editorial, saw that
page.

01:03:52.319 --> 01:03:55.770
And so it was like, "Oh, he's the big giant
dice guy."

01:03:55.770 --> 01:04:01.210
And the reason I was doing giant dice and
doing all these crazy flamboyant layouts in

01:04:01.210 --> 01:04:06.200
Infinity Inc., because Tim, my drawing was
mediocre.

01:04:06.200 --> 01:04:07.200
I knew that.

01:04:07.200 --> 01:04:10.010
At some point, you've got to be realistic
about your skills.

01:04:10.010 --> 01:04:12.460
I was mediocre and I had two choices.

01:04:12.460 --> 01:04:20.530
I could either put mediocre drawings and boring
layouts or I could be flamboyant and baffle

01:04:20.530 --> 01:04:27.529
them with my BS and get them to look at all
this sort of window trimming and not pay attention

01:04:27.529 --> 01:04:30.130
that maybe I'm drawing my eyes crooked.

01:04:30.130 --> 01:04:31.680
And it worked and it worked.

01:04:31.680 --> 01:04:33.059
People were like, "Oh, my gosh."

01:04:33.059 --> 01:04:38.240
And so by the time I left Infinity, I think
I had risen to the point that I was voted

01:04:38.240 --> 01:04:41.160
the fifth most popular penciler at that point.

01:04:41.160 --> 01:04:43.069
I'm like, "Wow."

01:04:43.069 --> 01:04:46.720
I go over to Marvel and then they say, which
was weird because Marvel was always the house

01:04:46.720 --> 01:04:51.920
of ideas, and at this point they had flopped
with DC and they were boring.

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:55.049
And they said, "You can come, but you can't
do the giant dice style."

01:04:55.049 --> 01:04:56.550
And I go, "What do you want me to do?"

01:04:56.550 --> 01:04:58.050
And they go, "We just want you to do a grid."

01:04:58.050 --> 01:05:01.680
Now, let me just tell you, anybody listening,
that means you take a piece of paper and you

01:05:01.680 --> 01:05:05.250
divide it into six equal panels and you go.

01:05:05.250 --> 01:05:08.309
It is the most boring, easiest thing to do.

01:05:08.309 --> 01:05:12.890
Essentially they took a guy like me, who I
thought was an artistic sprinter, and they

01:05:12.890 --> 01:05:15.510
said, "Could you put lead boots on?"

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:16.520
Shoot.

01:05:16.520 --> 01:05:18.319
This is going to be easy, right?

01:05:18.319 --> 01:05:22.510
I don't know why you want to do it, but it
was frustrating because there was no artistic

01:05:22.510 --> 01:05:24.789
freedom to do it.

01:05:24.789 --> 01:05:25.789
I did it.

01:05:25.789 --> 01:05:29.251
They give me the first job back at Marvel
and they go, "Hey, problem, you usually have

01:05:29.251 --> 01:05:34.110
30 days to do a book, once a month, but we're
behind the eight ball, we can give you this

01:05:34.110 --> 01:05:35.589
job but you've only got 10 days."

01:05:35.589 --> 01:05:37.339
I gave it to them in eight.

01:05:37.339 --> 01:05:38.339
Was it my best job?

01:05:38.339 --> 01:05:40.790
Of course it wasn't, but was it done in eight?

01:05:40.790 --> 01:05:41.790
Yeah.

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:44.569
Did it get me brownie points right on the
spot?

01:05:44.569 --> 01:05:47.170
They were amazed I gave it to them in eight
days.

01:05:47.170 --> 01:05:50.430
Boom, within weeks they're offering me The
Incredible Hulk.

01:05:50.430 --> 01:05:55.400
Now, once you get to Incredible Hulk, this
is the next step in climbing the ladder.

01:05:55.400 --> 01:06:00.440
All of a sudden, look, I've been in the business
probably two, three, four years at that point.

01:06:00.440 --> 01:06:04.579
And when I get The Incredible Hulk, finally
I hear the words from my mom and dad, "Oh,

01:06:04.579 --> 01:06:06.690
my God, you finally made it."

01:06:06.690 --> 01:06:09.960
And the reason was because they'd heard of
The Hulk.

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:13.960
Up to then, everything I was doing was like,
"I don't know if he is ever going to make

01:06:13.960 --> 01:06:14.960
it."

01:06:14.960 --> 01:06:16.920
I was still working at Marvel and DC, it just
wasn't for books.

01:06:16.920 --> 01:06:22.801
But if you can go to the neighbors' Halloween
party and say, "My son draws The Hulk," they've

01:06:22.801 --> 01:06:26.140
heard of the word Hulk too, so all of a sudden
it's like, "Oh, my gosh, your son.

01:06:26.140 --> 01:06:28.410
He must be good."

01:06:28.410 --> 01:06:33.020
It's important to have characters that you
know because it helps the relatives in your

01:06:33.020 --> 01:06:36.970
circle to think that you're a bigger shot
than you are, even though it's the exact same

01:06:36.970 --> 01:06:40.150
amount of work and the same pay on top of
it.

01:06:40.150 --> 01:06:45.589
Okay, so you get to the next step and now
you're going hot.

01:06:45.589 --> 01:06:48.809
Now you're just fighting for the next step
up.

01:06:48.809 --> 01:06:53.819
And I'll quickly sort of get you to this,
and I know I'm long winded in all your questions.

01:06:53.819 --> 01:06:55.609
I wish I was better, that I —
Tim Ferriss: I'm into it.

01:06:55.609 --> 01:06:56.609
Todd McFarlane: Okay.

01:06:56.609 --> 01:06:57.609
Tim Ferriss: Run with it.

01:06:57.609 --> 01:06:58.609
Run with it.

01:06:58.609 --> 01:06:59.609
Todd McFarlane: I do The Hulk.

01:06:59.609 --> 01:07:02.299
I'm doing penciling and then I go, "I'm going
to do inking," because at this point I got

01:07:02.299 --> 01:07:03.809
fast enough that I could do two books.

01:07:03.809 --> 01:07:05.589
There wasn't too many people that could do
two books.

01:07:05.589 --> 01:07:07.020
I was doing two books.

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:09.660
So they give me a second book.

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:12.380
The same editor who gave me The Incredible
Hulk gives me another book.

01:07:12.380 --> 01:07:13.380
It's called G.I.

01:07:13.380 --> 01:07:14.550
Joe: A Real American Hero.

01:07:14.550 --> 01:07:19.539
Now, the thing that's sort of ironic about
that is I was living up in Canada and it never

01:07:19.539 --> 01:07:23.880
really sort — I was going, "Wow, if they
only knew that this Canadian" — and I'm

01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:28.200
Canadian, up here in Canada — "[is] drawing
their real American heroes, this might be

01:07:28.200 --> 01:07:29.980
a bit of a problem," but nobody's going to
tell.

01:07:29.980 --> 01:07:32.200
There was no internet.

01:07:32.200 --> 01:07:34.090
I did it, the first issue of G.I.

01:07:34.090 --> 01:07:38.610
Joe, and I was doing The Hulk, and I get a
phone call and I was going back and forth

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:44.460
with this writer and we were bashing heads
every single page of that.

01:07:44.460 --> 01:07:49.280
And finally after the first issue, my editor
phones up and says, "Todd, I've got to let

01:07:49.280 --> 01:07:50.280
you go."

01:07:50.280 --> 01:07:51.280
I'm like, "One issue?"

01:07:51.280 --> 01:07:52.490
I'm one issue into G.I.

01:07:52.490 --> 01:07:53.490
Joe.

01:07:53.490 --> 01:07:58.230
I'm making my marks on The Hulk because now
I'm in the top three of some of the voting

01:07:58.230 --> 01:07:59.930
artistically.

01:07:59.930 --> 01:08:00.930
And I go, "What?

01:08:00.930 --> 01:08:02.060
You're going to fire me?"

01:08:02.060 --> 01:08:08.920
But let me just tell you, it was a relief
because it was such a pain for that one month.

01:08:08.920 --> 01:08:14.150
And that my view of comic books and the writer's
view of comic books were so diametrically

01:08:14.150 --> 01:08:15.299
opposed.

01:08:15.299 --> 01:08:17.429
I just —
Tim Ferriss: Where did you guys clash on that?

01:08:17.429 --> 01:08:18.870
What kind of decisions or what type of stuff?

01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:20.949
Todd McFarlane: Well, he probably sees it
a little bit differently, but I'll just give

01:08:20.949 --> 01:08:21.949
you my point of view.

01:08:21.949 --> 01:08:26.100
I assumed the people that were reading the
comic books had eyes and brains.

01:08:26.100 --> 01:08:29.020
He assumed, from my perspective, they didn't.

01:08:29.020 --> 01:08:34.679
Because he said it, "Assume your reader isn't,"
and it's his words, not mine, "Assume the

01:08:34.679 --> 01:08:42.350
reader is an aboriginal bushman and he just
came out of the tundra in Australia and he's

01:08:42.350 --> 01:08:43.969
never seen a comic book.

01:08:43.969 --> 01:08:48.950
Your storytelling must be that clear," which
basically meant you can't have somebody walk

01:08:48.950 --> 01:08:55.190
into a house and then cut to them inside the
house or even closing the door on the inside.

01:08:55.190 --> 01:09:00.040
You have to grab the door, open the door,
walk in the room.

01:09:00.040 --> 01:09:03.069
And I just went, "Larry, I guess I see the
world differently.

01:09:03.069 --> 01:09:10.109
I assume, silly me, that the people reading
the comic books watch fucking TV, go to movies,

01:09:10.109 --> 01:09:11.430
read books.

01:09:11.430 --> 01:09:14.480
They understand how stories are told."

01:09:14.480 --> 01:09:20.449
And as a matter of fact, on a movie when you
cut scenes, they don't even have a caption

01:09:20.449 --> 01:09:22.960
in 99.9 percent of the time.

01:09:22.960 --> 01:09:28.600
People just know that it's different people
in a different setting, it must be a scene

01:09:28.600 --> 01:09:29.600
cut.

01:09:29.600 --> 01:09:30.600
You don't have to do it.

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:33.469
But anyways, he had his way of seeing the
world.

01:09:33.469 --> 01:09:35.600
I had my way of seeing the world.

01:09:35.600 --> 01:09:37.339
Fine, no big deal.

01:09:37.339 --> 01:09:38.949
So, he fires me off it.

01:09:38.949 --> 01:09:45.180
And I remember, I was sitting in my apartment
up in Vancouver and I leaned back and I looked

01:09:45.180 --> 01:09:49.140
at the clock and it was 12:06 and I was a
little bit bombed because I'm like, "Man,

01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:53.390
I've never been fired."

01:09:53.390 --> 01:09:54.650
It was, "Oh, man."

01:09:54.650 --> 01:09:55.680
At 12:14, "Dring."

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:58.650
I am telling you, no lie, seven minutes.

01:09:58.650 --> 01:10:00.739
I was unemployed for seven minutes.

01:10:00.739 --> 01:10:01.739
Dring.

01:10:01.739 --> 01:10:02.739
"Todd?"

01:10:02.739 --> 01:10:03.739
Yeah.

01:10:03.739 --> 01:10:05.300
"Hey, this is Dick Giordano over at DC."

01:10:05.300 --> 01:10:10.230
Now, remember I'd left DC to go back over
to Marvel, "You remember when you left you

01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:14.179
said the only reason you would come back is
to draw Batman?"

01:10:14.179 --> 01:10:15.179
"Yeah."

01:10:15.179 --> 01:10:19.610
"Well, we've got this book and it's..."

01:10:19.610 --> 01:10:23.180
First there was Batman: The Dark Night, then
there was Batman: Year One, and they had another

01:10:23.180 --> 01:10:24.180
project.

01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:25.199
It was called Batman: Year Two.

01:10:25.199 --> 01:10:27.650
"We've got a book called Batman: Year Two.

01:10:27.650 --> 01:10:32.920
And the artist, it's a four-part story, and
the artist quit after the first one.

01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:35.360
Can you finish the last three?"

01:10:35.360 --> 01:10:37.030
"What?

01:10:37.030 --> 01:10:41.449
You're saying my choice right now is either
stay on, which I didn't have an option, but

01:10:41.449 --> 01:10:42.449
stay on G.I.

01:10:42.449 --> 01:10:48.830
Joe, that I just got fired from, that was
basically putting daggers in my eyes, or do

01:10:48.830 --> 01:10:49.830
Batman?

01:10:49.830 --> 01:10:51.540
That was the only character I'd come back
for?"

01:10:51.540 --> 01:10:55.030
So, I jumped on Batman: Year Two.

01:10:55.030 --> 01:11:00.260
Now, it gets a little crazier by that point
because at this point I was only being the

01:11:00.260 --> 01:11:01.760
pencil artist.

01:11:01.760 --> 01:11:05.920
And in comic books, they'd bring in another
person who is the inker.

01:11:05.920 --> 01:11:07.570
Some people call him the tracer.

01:11:07.570 --> 01:11:08.739
It's not true.

01:11:08.739 --> 01:11:10.969
Good inkers add a lot to pages.

01:11:10.969 --> 01:11:16.989
But I thought I had — my drawing style is
what I would call sort of this new wave '90

01:11:16.989 --> 01:11:17.989
style.

01:11:17.989 --> 01:11:25.220
And I was constantly getting inkers that were
like these 1960s old school brush guys.

01:11:25.220 --> 01:11:29.989
I was drawing in a way that I thought you
should be using a pen because there's a different

01:11:29.989 --> 01:11:31.070
technique with it.

01:11:31.070 --> 01:11:38.130
And they kept putting brush guys on me, and
it was so transforming.

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:39.940
They literally would bury the artwork.

01:11:39.940 --> 01:11:45.350
If you saw what I was drawing, you saw the
printed page, it was, to me, night and day.

01:11:45.350 --> 01:11:48.080
I do this, the part two of Batman: Year Two.

01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:52.170
Then I do part three and then they send me
the samples and I finally, again, another

01:11:52.170 --> 01:11:57.989
one of those moments in my life with clarity,
that I look at it and I see this flash page

01:11:57.989 --> 01:12:02.840
and this flash page has a Commissioner Gordon
and he's holding the gun.

01:12:02.840 --> 01:12:04.820
And to me it looks hairy, right?

01:12:04.820 --> 01:12:05.820
I go, "It's metallic.

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:06.820
Why is it looking hairy?"

01:12:06.820 --> 01:12:14.340
And it's because the inker was doing these
lines that worked for him, I guess.

01:12:14.340 --> 01:12:19.090
And then I looked at another panel and it
was the hallway.

01:12:19.090 --> 01:12:24.070
And when you're doing a down shot of a hallway,
the lines get closer.

01:12:24.070 --> 01:12:25.070
It's basically perspective.

01:12:25.070 --> 01:12:28.320
I won't bore people, but it's just the illusion
of depth.

01:12:28.320 --> 01:12:34.150
And I'd done all these drawings or these lines
to give that illusion and they were horizontal.

01:12:34.150 --> 01:12:38.280
And then I looked at the panel and he had
done the exact same thing, it was brilliant,

01:12:38.280 --> 01:12:40.969
except he did them vertically.

01:12:40.969 --> 01:12:44.090
And so it was these moments.

01:12:44.090 --> 01:12:49.100
And so it wasn't that one was better than
the other.

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:51.420
They both worked.

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:55.630
Again, I just try to get as simple as possible.

01:12:55.630 --> 01:13:00.650
The reason, and I don't know if anybody could
understand this concept, but the reason I

01:13:00.650 --> 01:13:08.330
made them go up and down vertically was because,
silly me, I fucking wanted them to go up and

01:13:08.330 --> 01:13:10.070
down vertically.

01:13:10.070 --> 01:13:15.810
Because if I had wanted them to go horizontally,
which was another option, I would've drawn

01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:16.810
them.

01:13:16.810 --> 01:13:21.760
I can draw horizontal lines equally as well
as vertical lines.

01:13:21.760 --> 01:13:25.830
The reason they were vertical, because I must
have meant horizontal.

01:13:25.830 --> 01:13:27.100
That's why I did.

01:13:27.100 --> 01:13:29.130
What are you talking about?

01:13:29.130 --> 01:13:34.190
Just copy the lines and do your thing with
the lines that are there.

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:40.740
And so I phoned the editor and I go, "Hey,
Denny, I don't mean to do this to you because

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:41.910
you already lost one artist.

01:13:41.910 --> 01:13:44.081
And I don't mean to do power play because
I don't like doing that.

01:13:44.081 --> 01:13:45.330
I don't want to be that guy.

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:46.540
But here's the gig.

01:13:46.540 --> 01:13:52.310
If I can't ink the last chapter of this book,
and let me be completely honest, I've never

01:13:52.310 --> 01:13:53.310
inked before.

01:13:53.310 --> 01:13:55.610
I'm a complete noob.

01:13:55.610 --> 01:13:58.550
I can't do this, I can't do this.

01:13:58.550 --> 01:14:02.610
I can't have people literally going in the
opposite direction of my artwork.

01:14:02.610 --> 01:14:04.760
I can't do it."

01:14:04.760 --> 01:14:06.080
He was in a tough spot.

01:14:06.080 --> 01:14:09.600
He was like, "Oh, okay, Paul and Todd, can
you get it done by the deadline?"

01:14:09.600 --> 01:14:10.790
Yeah, sure.

01:14:10.790 --> 01:14:12.870
I did my first inking.

01:14:12.870 --> 01:14:16.489
Thanks to all those inkers for turning me
into an inker.

01:14:16.489 --> 01:14:18.770
And from there on out, I was my own inker.

01:14:18.770 --> 01:14:20.660
I started inking The Hulk.

01:14:20.660 --> 01:14:25.000
If you look at it, I went back to my editor
on The Hulk and I go, "Well, they let me ink

01:14:25.000 --> 01:14:26.080
the Batman."

01:14:26.080 --> 01:14:29.250
And so he is like, "Oh, okay, I guess you
can ink The Hulk."

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:32.630
And now I'm going to eventually get to your
question about Venom.

01:14:32.630 --> 01:14:33.780
It was a long winded way.

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:34.780
Sorry.

01:14:34.780 --> 01:14:35.780
Eventually —
Tim Ferriss: That's okay.

01:14:35.780 --> 01:14:37.360
Let me pause you for just a quick sec.

01:14:37.360 --> 01:14:42.150
For people who have no context on your art,
I mean, very fine details.

01:14:42.150 --> 01:14:47.920
And when you're talking about the older school
inking, lots of thick black, I mean, lots

01:14:47.920 --> 01:14:48.920
of —
Todd McFarlane: You simplify.

01:14:48.920 --> 01:14:49.920
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:14:49.920 --> 01:14:50.920
So, you simplified, right?

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:51.920
Todd McFarlane: Right.

01:14:51.920 --> 01:14:55.090
Tim Ferriss: When you went from penciling
to, "I need to ink this or I'm out," what

01:14:55.090 --> 01:14:59.190
were the biggest differences between penciling
and inking?

01:14:59.190 --> 01:15:05.111
As someone who's never inked, I'd be curious
to know what you learned or felt or if it

01:15:05.111 --> 01:15:06.830
just mapped over really easily.

01:15:06.830 --> 01:15:08.650
What was it like to do your first ink?

01:15:08.650 --> 01:15:13.410
Todd McFarlane: Tim, here's the first shock
that sometimes my enthusiasm gets the better

01:15:13.410 --> 01:15:18.520
of me, and there's nobody that hates Todd
more than Todd at times because it's like,

01:15:18.520 --> 01:15:21.520
"What are you doing, Todd?"

01:15:21.520 --> 01:15:23.139
Then I learn the magic.

01:15:23.139 --> 01:15:25.730
I'm now going to do two jobs.

01:15:25.730 --> 01:15:28.730
If you pencil a book, you get 30 days.

01:15:28.730 --> 01:15:31.780
If you ink a book — I pencil it, they give
me 30 days.

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:35.120
Then they hand the pages to you, Tim, you
ink it, you get 30 days.

01:15:35.120 --> 01:15:40.300
If I want to pencil and ink, they don't give
me 30 plus 30, right?

01:15:40.300 --> 01:15:43.030
If there's two of us, it's 30 plus 30.

01:15:43.030 --> 01:15:44.610
I get 30, you get 30.

01:15:44.610 --> 01:15:48.710
They're going, "You can do two jobs, Todd,
but you've still only got 30 days."

01:15:48.710 --> 01:15:53.660
Essentially you're doing twice the work and
you have to do it twice as fast because the

01:15:53.660 --> 01:15:55.030
book still comes out.

01:15:55.030 --> 01:16:00.290
And so, lesson learned, if you're going to
pick up more work, you might want to ask how

01:16:00.290 --> 01:16:02.270
much extra time do you have?

01:16:02.270 --> 01:16:06.460
And when the answer is zero, you might want
to rethink your ask.

01:16:06.460 --> 01:16:12.040
So because eventually I got to the point going,
"I'm going to write my own stories too," right?

01:16:12.040 --> 01:16:15.800
And that extra time also is zero.

01:16:15.800 --> 01:16:18.610
But that comes a little bit later in the career.

01:16:18.610 --> 01:16:22.920
So the first shock is I've now got to go faster.

01:16:22.920 --> 01:16:27.380
What it taught me personally, because now
I'm doing two books and I'm inking, right?

01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:31.880
So I'm a bit of a unicorn at this point because
very few people can even pencil two books,

01:16:31.880 --> 01:16:35.030
was I had to create for myself efficiencies.

01:16:35.030 --> 01:16:39.140
And this goes into business, right?

01:16:39.140 --> 01:16:42.719
And even though we're doing art, this is just
efficiencies.

01:16:42.719 --> 01:16:47.409
So I do the efficiency and I go, "I don't
have time to do a lot of what they call under

01:16:47.409 --> 01:16:48.409
drawing."

01:16:48.409 --> 01:16:50.430
I've got to literally draw with ink.

01:16:50.430 --> 01:16:52.290
I don't have time to do the job twice.

01:16:52.290 --> 01:16:54.640
I can't pencil and then ink my own work.

01:16:54.640 --> 01:17:00.170
I have to do it all in one fell swoop, which
is horrifying to people who've never inked

01:17:00.170 --> 01:17:01.290
themselves.

01:17:01.290 --> 01:17:06.840
Because every person I've shown amongst some
amazing peers, amongst some amazing peers,

01:17:06.840 --> 01:17:11.300
when they see what my process is, they go,
"I can't even make out what's on your page."

01:17:11.300 --> 01:17:14.560
And I go, "That's okay, I'm just going to
finish it with the ink."

01:17:14.560 --> 01:17:17.120
And they go, "Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no,
no you can't go with the ink."

01:17:17.120 --> 01:17:18.120
I'm like, "Why?"

01:17:18.120 --> 01:17:19.120
"Because it's permanent."

01:17:19.120 --> 01:17:20.989
I'm like, "What if you make a mistake?"

01:17:20.989 --> 01:17:23.750
"There's a thing called Wite-Out?

01:17:23.750 --> 01:17:24.810
You just white it out."

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:27.409
And they're going, "Yeah, but what if you
make another mistake?"

01:17:27.409 --> 01:17:28.490
"I just use Wite-Out."

01:17:28.490 --> 01:17:30.360
"Let me ask you a question in reverse.

01:17:30.360 --> 01:17:32.230
What happens if you draw in pencil and you
make a mistake?"

01:17:32.230 --> 01:17:33.860
"Oh, I just use an eraser."

01:17:33.860 --> 01:17:36.430
"Well why can you use an eraser and I can't
use Wite-Out?

01:17:36.430 --> 01:17:37.530
It's the same thing."

01:17:37.530 --> 01:17:39.070
"Yeah, but it's ink."

01:17:39.070 --> 01:17:42.650
And it literally was this mental wall for
people.

01:17:42.650 --> 01:17:47.590
And these are people that I would sit next
to at a convention, that would do 20 sketches

01:17:47.590 --> 01:17:50.120
with a pencil and never erase one line.

01:17:50.120 --> 01:17:52.990
And I used to turn to them and go, "Why couldn't
you have done that with ink?"

01:17:52.990 --> 01:17:54.360
And their answer was always the same.

01:17:54.360 --> 01:17:55.710
"What if I made a mistake?"

01:17:55.710 --> 01:17:56.710
"You didn't.

01:17:56.710 --> 01:17:59.130
I've been watching you for four hours, you
haven't made one mistake.

01:17:59.130 --> 01:18:02.801
Why is it if I changed the tool and somehow
you're going to make a mistake?

01:18:02.801 --> 01:18:06.770
But you know what, you do you, I'll do my
thing."

01:18:06.770 --> 01:18:14.770
And so I had to just pull back the drawing
and figure it out in one step so I could keep

01:18:14.770 --> 01:18:15.770
the deadline.

01:18:15.770 --> 01:18:18.800
That was my learning experience.

01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:22.600
Now, question number two, did I hit the ground
running?

01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:23.679
Of course I didn't.

01:18:23.679 --> 01:18:24.960
Of course I didn't.

01:18:24.960 --> 01:18:30.820
But if you look at my inking at the beginning,
it's very, very crude.

01:18:30.820 --> 01:18:34.570
And even on Spider-Man, which is the book
The Catapult, if you look at the thickness

01:18:34.570 --> 01:18:40.760
of the webs on his costume, you will see a
noticeable difference, I think, from issue

01:18:40.760 --> 01:18:43.230
300 when I started inking Spider-Man.

01:18:43.230 --> 01:18:49.949
And if you look at about issue 320 for sure,
by the time I get on the new Spider-Man book

01:18:49.949 --> 01:18:54.150
that I'm drawing, for sure there's a dramatic
difference visually.

01:18:54.150 --> 01:18:56.620
So I was constantly learning that trade.

01:18:56.620 --> 01:19:02.080
It was Todd, the professional who'd been penciling
for five, six years, and Todd, the newbie

01:19:02.080 --> 01:19:03.160
inker.

01:19:03.160 --> 01:19:06.770
I wasn't going to be a five- or six-year vet
inking, I was a new inker.

01:19:06.770 --> 01:19:11.800
So I had to learn that trade and catch up
those five or six years that the other half

01:19:11.800 --> 01:19:15.320
of my brain had already sort of tackled with
penciling.

01:19:15.320 --> 01:19:16.320
So go ahead.

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:17.320
Because eventually now this going to get me
to Venom.

01:19:17.320 --> 01:19:18.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, to Venom.

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:19.320
Yeah.

01:19:19.320 --> 01:19:20.320
Yeah.

01:19:20.320 --> 01:19:21.320
Todd McFarlane: So very quickly.

01:19:21.320 --> 01:19:22.320
Tim Ferriss: Fire away.

01:19:22.320 --> 01:19:24.350
Todd McFarlane: So then I finished the Batman
project and now I'm back down to only one

01:19:24.350 --> 01:19:26.120
book so I can do two books.

01:19:26.120 --> 01:19:30.900
And so again, I'm looking for another book
and all the editors at Marvel said, "Hey,

01:19:30.900 --> 01:19:31.900
yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:19:31.900 --> 01:19:32.900
Come and talk to me.

01:19:32.900 --> 01:19:38.230
But whatever you do, don't go into the Spider-Man
office because it's a shambles."

01:19:38.230 --> 01:19:45.940
Now you may or may not have gathered that
Todd doesn't seem to color inside the lines

01:19:45.940 --> 01:19:47.430
a lot of the times.

01:19:47.430 --> 01:19:52.230
So you don't tell me, "Don't go into that
office," right?

01:19:52.230 --> 01:19:53.670
Because to me —
Tim Ferriss: Unless you want Todd to go into

01:19:53.670 --> 01:19:54.670
the office.

01:19:54.670 --> 01:19:55.750
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, what are you guys doing?

01:19:55.750 --> 01:20:04.150
So I went into the office and it was the editor,
Jim Salicrup at that time, a good man, was

01:20:04.150 --> 01:20:05.590
losing and turning over artists.

01:20:05.590 --> 01:20:08.489
The books were in a bit of a sales decline.

01:20:08.489 --> 01:20:12.510
Like I said, I picked up the book, it was
like at number 21, 22.

01:20:12.510 --> 01:20:14.610
And I said, "Hey, I can do another book."

01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:20.810
And we had a chat and I said, "I want to ink
the book."

01:20:20.810 --> 01:20:23.310
And then they're like, "Well, maybe in a couple
of months."

01:20:23.310 --> 01:20:25.219
And I go, "I'm inking The Hulk," right?

01:20:25.219 --> 01:20:28.370
"Come on, go talk to Bob, the editor on that
book."

01:20:28.370 --> 01:20:31.330
And he's like, "Well, just give me a couple
of months.

01:20:31.330 --> 01:20:33.770
How about starting at 300, you can ink the
book."

01:20:33.770 --> 01:20:37.340
And I'm like, "Yeah, okay, we'll do it."

01:20:37.340 --> 01:20:43.670
But the other piece of it was, oh, this one
other sort of slight problem.

01:20:43.670 --> 01:20:45.040
Spider-Man's got a black costume.

01:20:45.040 --> 01:20:50.160
And this is a costume that was created for
this book called Secret War, which is the

01:20:50.160 --> 01:20:53.620
black costume with the white spider on it.

01:20:53.620 --> 01:20:56.310
And I go, "That's not Spider-Man to me.

01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:57.440
Maybe I'm just old school.

01:20:57.440 --> 01:21:01.940
Spider-Man's that guy in the blue and the
red with the webs on it, Spider-Man.

01:21:01.940 --> 01:21:07.750
So can we just get rid of that black costume
and get back to Spider-Man?"

01:21:07.750 --> 01:21:11.050
And then this is sort of the happy accident.

01:21:11.050 --> 01:21:17.010
He said, "Well, the editor in chief really
likes the black costume and I don't think

01:21:17.010 --> 01:21:18.170
he's going to go for that.

01:21:18.170 --> 01:21:20.420
So he is not going to want to get rid of it.

01:21:20.420 --> 01:21:24.699
He had something to do with Secret Wars and
they're digging it."

01:21:24.699 --> 01:21:29.090
And I went, "Man, I don't want to draw Spider-Man
in the black costume.

01:21:29.090 --> 01:21:31.170
It's like doing Batman in polka dots.

01:21:31.170 --> 01:21:32.929
Doesn't make any sense to me.

01:21:32.929 --> 01:21:35.870
So what if I come back to you with some designs?

01:21:35.870 --> 01:21:41.401
We just rip the costume off, we put it on
somebody, I'll create another character, give

01:21:41.401 --> 01:21:44.679
it to the writers we just figured out and
then we still have the black costume and then

01:21:44.679 --> 01:21:48.670
we can get the red and blue back on Peter
Parker and it's a win, win, win."

01:21:48.670 --> 01:21:50.090
He was like, "Okay, that might work."

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:55.740
So I go away, I do the drawing, the costume
was alive.

01:21:55.740 --> 01:21:57.650
So I go, "Oh, it must be an alien."

01:21:57.650 --> 01:22:02.490
So I created this big giant hulking alien
and gave him the big eyes and the slobbering

01:22:02.490 --> 01:22:03.490
teeth.

01:22:03.490 --> 01:22:05.300
And to me, it was a gorilla.

01:22:05.300 --> 01:22:06.640
It's like an alien gorilla.

01:22:06.640 --> 01:22:08.660
And then the claws and everything else.

01:22:08.660 --> 01:22:10.699
And that was a design for Venom, like, "Here."

01:22:10.699 --> 01:22:12.240
We didn't have a name at that point.

01:22:12.240 --> 01:22:13.480
I'd just go, "Here.

01:22:13.480 --> 01:22:15.810
Here's the new bad guy, here it is.

01:22:15.810 --> 01:22:16.810
Go."

01:22:16.810 --> 01:22:19.120
And they looked at it and went, "Oh, that's
cool.

01:22:19.120 --> 01:22:20.400
We'll give it to the writer."

01:22:20.400 --> 01:22:23.420
And so they gave it to the writer, they cleared
it through upper management.

01:22:23.420 --> 01:22:26.320
They said, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, that seems like
a reasonable thing."

01:22:26.320 --> 01:22:30.430
The writer comes back to me and says, "Todd,
the guy is Eddie Brock."

01:22:30.430 --> 01:22:33.400
And I went, "Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa."

01:22:33.400 --> 01:22:34.510
The writer's name was David.

01:22:34.510 --> 01:22:36.300
"Whoa, David, Eddie Brock?

01:22:36.300 --> 01:22:37.670
Eddie Brock's a human.

01:22:37.670 --> 01:22:39.659
Did you see my design?"

01:22:39.659 --> 01:22:42.389
Information I could have used earlier.

01:22:42.389 --> 01:22:46.820
I would've designed it differently if I knew
it was a human that I was putting the black

01:22:46.820 --> 01:22:48.219
costume on.

01:22:48.219 --> 01:22:50.050
But I sort of liked the design.

01:22:50.050 --> 01:22:55.210
I thought it was cool and it was giant and
I thought that it would be more formidable

01:22:55.210 --> 01:23:00.020
for Spider-Man to go up against something
that was way bigger than him, than another

01:23:00.020 --> 01:23:01.880
human humanoid form.

01:23:01.880 --> 01:23:05.570
And this is sort of the geeky stuff that us
creators go through.

01:23:05.570 --> 01:23:11.620
And so I said, "But man, if Bruce Banner,
little shunt, can turn into The Hulk, then

01:23:11.620 --> 01:23:16.969
by gosh, why can't Eddie Brock somehow be
buried in this costume somewhere," right?

01:23:16.969 --> 01:23:21.030
So we never sort of wavered from it.

01:23:21.030 --> 01:23:27.760
Venom comes out, has a big play in issue 300,
Amazing Spider-Man 300.

01:23:27.760 --> 01:23:28.860
Sales go crazy.

01:23:28.860 --> 01:23:33.610
We knew we had something on our hands because
every time Venom kept coming back the mail,

01:23:33.610 --> 01:23:35.199
again, there was no internet.

01:23:35.199 --> 01:23:37.620
But the mail kept getting bigger and people
were like, "Oh, my gosh.

01:23:37.620 --> 01:23:38.620
Oh, my gosh.

01:23:38.620 --> 01:23:39.620
Oh, my gosh."

01:23:39.620 --> 01:23:46.880
And so now, fast forward with hindsight and
Venom is a worldwide brand, made a billion

01:23:46.880 --> 01:23:49.140
dollars, we saw it in movies.

01:23:49.140 --> 01:23:51.659
So again, there's the happy accident.

01:23:51.659 --> 01:23:56.060
And if you look at issue 300, if you want
to go buy it, it comes with a couple of things.

01:23:56.060 --> 01:23:57.730
One, it's an anniversary book.

01:23:57.730 --> 01:23:58.989
Sales have gone way up on it.

01:23:58.989 --> 01:24:00.530
One, it's an anniversary book.

01:24:00.530 --> 01:24:02.330
Issue 300, Amazing Spider-Man.

01:24:02.330 --> 01:24:03.360
Good buy.

01:24:03.360 --> 01:24:10.870
Some of the very first early work of me on
Spider-Man and my first inking job on Spider-Man.

01:24:10.870 --> 01:24:16.030
But more importantly, it's the origin of Venom,
right?

01:24:16.030 --> 01:24:18.131
And so for people, they spend hundreds of
dollars.

01:24:18.131 --> 01:24:19.820
If you get these books, great, it's thousands
of dollars.

01:24:19.820 --> 01:24:21.389
So you can get the origin of Venom.

01:24:21.389 --> 01:24:22.870
I don't consider that book.

01:24:22.870 --> 01:24:25.699
If you were to ask me, "Is that the origin
of Venom?"

01:24:25.699 --> 01:24:26.699
"No.

01:24:26.699 --> 01:24:30.239
It's the issue, 'How do we get that black
costume off Peter Parker so I can draw the

01:24:30.239 --> 01:24:34.500
classic red and blue costume because that's
Spider-Man to me,'" right?

01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:37.520
Venom, I didn't care at that moment about
Venom.

01:24:37.520 --> 01:24:38.739
It was like I get rid of it.

01:24:38.739 --> 01:24:43.239
The last page of that issue, Peter Parker
gets rid of the black costume because he has

01:24:43.239 --> 01:24:44.239
a fight with Venom.

01:24:44.239 --> 01:24:48.290
Venom goes on his way and he pulls a box out
from underneath the bed and he pulls out the

01:24:48.290 --> 01:24:52.340
classic uniform, the red and blue, the one
that to me is Spider-Man.

01:24:52.340 --> 01:24:57.840
And the last page, which I still have today
because it was like, "Finally, I'm drawing

01:24:57.840 --> 01:24:58.840
Spider-Man."

01:24:58.840 --> 01:25:01.320
Because I started on issue 298.

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:02.320
No black costume.

01:25:02.320 --> 01:25:03.860
299, no black costume.

01:25:03.860 --> 01:25:07.219
And every page, but one of issue 300.

01:25:07.219 --> 01:25:11.929
But that last page, it was — and it even
says, I think the caption, it says, "And the

01:25:11.929 --> 01:25:12.929
new beginning."

01:25:12.929 --> 01:25:15.699
And to me I go, "Finally, I get to draw Spider-Man."

01:25:15.699 --> 01:25:17.429
This was for me, finally.

01:25:17.429 --> 01:25:22.139
Now somehow Venom was the byproduct of that,
right?

01:25:22.139 --> 01:25:24.489
Now here's what should happen, Tim.

01:25:24.489 --> 01:25:28.580
An employee should come into the office, they
should say, "No, he's wearing the black costume.

01:25:28.580 --> 01:25:34.659
We want to give you the job on Amazing Spider-Man,"
one of the granddaddy books of the company.

01:25:34.659 --> 01:25:38.210
And most sane employees will go, "Yes sir,
yes ma'am.

01:25:38.210 --> 01:25:40.270
When is the book due?"

01:25:40.270 --> 01:25:45.120
I don't know, Todd is Todd and ya ya yay,
right?

01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:50.160
And so I just was like, "No, I need the red
and blue costume."

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:58.580
But because of some of that arrogance, ego,
immaturity, whatever you want to call it,

01:25:58.580 --> 01:26:02.260
your by-product is you've got a character
called Venom that now creates Carnage and

01:26:02.260 --> 01:26:03.520
this whole slew.

01:26:03.520 --> 01:26:10.840
So if I was that guy, that employee, all of
that maybe never materializes, that's the

01:26:10.840 --> 01:26:13.030
true possibility of that.

01:26:13.030 --> 01:26:17.900
And then I just take all of that — remember,
Marvel's at their boring storytelling.

01:26:17.900 --> 01:26:22.550
I start pushing the boundaries of storytelling
to make it more, I thought, dynamic.

01:26:22.550 --> 01:26:25.210
I thought everything we do in comic books
is just a Broadway play.

01:26:25.210 --> 01:26:29.830
You must, everything should be big and you
should be talking and performing for the lady

01:26:29.830 --> 01:26:33.690
with bad hearing that's in the last row at
the theater.

01:26:33.690 --> 01:26:36.510
So that's what comic books are, bravado.

01:26:36.510 --> 01:26:43.020
And so I was doing all this fancy storytelling
and my editors were going, "Todd, you can't.

01:26:43.020 --> 01:26:45.540
You can't, you can't, you can't."

01:26:45.540 --> 01:26:47.090
And I'm like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, okay."

01:26:47.090 --> 01:26:48.360
But I just kept doing it.

01:26:48.360 --> 01:26:51.040
And then eventually, I was asking them and
I go, "Why can't we?"

01:26:51.040 --> 01:26:56.390
And they go, "Well the editor in chief, Jim
Shooter, he doesn't like that."

01:26:56.390 --> 01:27:01.950
I found that to be impossible to believe,
impossible to believe.

01:27:01.950 --> 01:27:07.540
Jim Shooter wants vanilla when he can have
tutti fruity or a banana split.

01:27:07.540 --> 01:27:09.400
Hard to believe.

01:27:09.400 --> 01:27:14.619
So we went and had a meeting one time, my
editor, Bob Harras, who ended up being the

01:27:14.619 --> 01:27:17.820
top dog at DC, I think he still is maybe.

01:27:17.820 --> 01:27:22.980
And we went into this meeting with Jim Shooter,
the editor in chief, where every editor was

01:27:22.980 --> 01:27:25.130
literally shaking in their boots from Jim
Shooter.

01:27:25.130 --> 01:27:28.179
He was like this authoritarian sort of figure.

01:27:28.179 --> 01:27:34.810
And as he's walking in the meeting, all Bob
says, "Don't ask him about storytelling, Todd,"

01:27:34.810 --> 01:27:36.540
because he just wanted to say hi to me.

01:27:36.540 --> 01:27:40.190
Because I was this new guy on The Hulk and
my career was starting to bud and he just

01:27:40.190 --> 01:27:41.190
wanted to say hi to me.

01:27:41.190 --> 01:27:43.179
It was just a casual conversation.

01:27:43.179 --> 01:27:45.140
We have a nice pleasant conversation.

01:27:45.140 --> 01:27:48.070
And then it's like he goes, "Okay, I've got
to get to my next meeting."

01:27:48.070 --> 01:27:49.070
And I'm like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah."

01:27:49.070 --> 01:27:52.670
And as we get up, because I wanted Bob to
be moving, I went, "Oh, hey Jim, just have

01:27:52.670 --> 01:27:54.989
one quick question.

01:27:54.989 --> 01:27:58.170
Can I change storytelling?

01:27:58.170 --> 01:28:02.210
Let me just ask you, am I allowed to have
characters burst out of the panels?

01:28:02.210 --> 01:28:05.920
Because that was what I was doing with Spider-Man,
and they're going, 'You can't, you can't.'"

01:28:05.920 --> 01:28:10.409
And he was like, looked at me inquisitively
and went, "Yeah, sure.

01:28:10.409 --> 01:28:11.409
Why?"

01:28:11.409 --> 01:28:13.780
"Okay, but can I do this other thing?"

01:28:13.780 --> 01:28:15.929
And he went, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure."

01:28:15.929 --> 01:28:18.739
And I went, "So it's okay for me to..."

01:28:18.739 --> 01:28:20.150
Because I go, "I think I heard somebody.

01:28:20.150 --> 01:28:23.150
It wasn't Bob my editor standing in front
of you.

01:28:23.150 --> 01:28:26.969
It must have been somebody else, told me that
somehow that you said you can't have things,

01:28:26.969 --> 01:28:31.030
you can't have panels overlapping and you
can't have characters punching out."

01:28:31.030 --> 01:28:33.900
And I walked them through a couple of things
and he was horrified.

01:28:33.900 --> 01:28:35.869
And he was like, "What?

01:28:35.869 --> 01:28:36.869
What are you saying, Todd?"

01:28:36.869 --> 01:28:40.260
And I'm going, "Yeah, yeah, that's just what
they're telling all the artists."

01:28:40.260 --> 01:28:41.389
And he was like, "No."

01:28:41.389 --> 01:28:43.170
And he got angry at that moment.

01:28:43.170 --> 01:28:45.200
He goes, "No, I never said that."

01:28:45.200 --> 01:28:47.540
He goes, "Here's what I said, 'You cannot...'"

01:28:47.540 --> 01:28:51.360
And I knew this was the answer, Tim, I knew
this was the answer.

01:28:51.360 --> 01:28:58.440
He said, "You can't do bad overlapping panels
and bad drawings of people jumping out."

01:28:58.440 --> 01:29:04.250
And then he explained to me the difference
between a bad version and a good version,

01:29:04.250 --> 01:29:05.250
right?

01:29:05.250 --> 01:29:07.580
And I knew what the bad and good version was.

01:29:07.580 --> 01:29:11.909
"Basically, don't have a guy jumping out of
a panel and you're covering up half the drawing

01:29:11.909 --> 01:29:13.520
of the next panel, right?

01:29:13.520 --> 01:29:17.320
As long as you're doing it in some negative
space, you are okay.

01:29:17.320 --> 01:29:21.120
Just as long as the storytelling's clear,
I don't care how you design it, Todd."

01:29:21.120 --> 01:29:22.159
I knew it.

01:29:22.159 --> 01:29:25.550
Ding, ding, ding, ding, ding, ding, ding,
ding, ding, and shoot.

01:29:25.550 --> 01:29:29.270
From that meeting on, you take a look at my
layouts in Spider-Man.

01:29:29.270 --> 01:29:31.130
It started getting crazier and crazier.

01:29:31.130 --> 01:29:33.639
Now, Jim Shooter gets pushed out very quickly.

01:29:33.639 --> 01:29:36.750
I keep doing my Todd thing on Spider-Man.

01:29:36.750 --> 01:29:41.340
And here's what I did on Spider-Man that literally
catapulted my career.

01:29:41.340 --> 01:29:43.219
It was a simple move.

01:29:43.219 --> 01:29:46.040
They were doing Spider-Man, emphasis on "Man."

01:29:46.040 --> 01:29:49.620
I flipped it to Spider-Man, emphasis on the
word "Spider."

01:29:49.620 --> 01:29:52.800
So when he put the costume on, I thought he
was an insect.

01:29:52.800 --> 01:29:55.940
And I didn't care about anatomy, I didn't
care whether it mattered.

01:29:55.940 --> 01:30:03.840
I just cared about the dynamics of this character
looking like a bug man and crawling in a way.

01:30:03.840 --> 01:30:08.030
And then as part of that, I added more webbing
on his costume.

01:30:08.030 --> 01:30:12.950
And then I had to come up with a new way of
doing his webs that have been done this way

01:30:12.950 --> 01:30:13.950
for 30 years.

01:30:13.950 --> 01:30:19.560
I go, "It doesn't work if you want to shoot
it towards camera, or if you want to create

01:30:19.560 --> 01:30:24.100
a false sense of volume, which is the only
thing we have as artists, you must give the

01:30:24.100 --> 01:30:27.510
illusion of 3D given that you're drawing on
a 2D piece of paper."

01:30:27.510 --> 01:30:32.389
So again, all these silly things that would
bore people, but I was doing it and oh, by

01:30:32.389 --> 01:30:34.190
the way, it fucking looks cool.

01:30:34.190 --> 01:30:35.400
Tim Ferriss: It doesn't bore me.

01:30:35.400 --> 01:30:36.940
No, this stuff is key.

01:30:36.940 --> 01:30:38.030
These are important decisions.

01:30:38.030 --> 01:30:39.193
Todd McFarlane: And so one thing in the moment,
so I go, "And it looks cool."

01:30:39.193 --> 01:30:41.760
And here's the moment I was talking about
earlier.

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:49.080
The moment you start messing with anybody's
icon, status quo comes into the equation and

01:30:49.080 --> 01:30:50.120
I'm now messing with it.

01:30:50.120 --> 01:30:54.830
I probably could have done what I did with
a lower tier character, but not Spider-Man.

01:30:54.830 --> 01:30:59.739
Spider-Man at this point, again at the Republic
company, is on their checks.

01:30:59.739 --> 01:31:01.489
Every check's got a little Spidey on it.

01:31:01.489 --> 01:31:09.200
He's on their quarterly reports, he's on their
internal memos and I'm messing with that look,

01:31:09.200 --> 01:31:10.200
right?

01:31:10.200 --> 01:31:17.250
And so they came and they were sitting there
and they took it as I was doing something,

01:31:17.250 --> 01:31:19.830
I thought they were doing it wrong and I was
right.

01:31:19.830 --> 01:31:21.239
No, no, no, no, no.

01:31:21.239 --> 01:31:22.321
Here is the reality of it.

01:31:22.321 --> 01:31:23.430
And I've said it plenty of times.

01:31:23.430 --> 01:31:28.179
I thought that the look that had been presented,
the classic look that was there, the one that

01:31:28.179 --> 01:31:32.350
everybody, if you close your eyes, you have
in your mind, if you're a certain age, was

01:31:32.350 --> 01:31:35.210
literally the Norman Rockwell version of Spider-Man.

01:31:35.210 --> 01:31:37.530
It was perfect.

01:31:37.530 --> 01:31:43.760
And the best I could hope for as a young budding
artist is to do a bad version of that and

01:31:43.760 --> 01:31:47.500
go, "Man, that's almost as cool as Norman
Rockwell's painting."

01:31:47.500 --> 01:31:50.260
Let me tell you, if you're going to be a painter,
never paint like Norman Rockwell.

01:31:50.260 --> 01:31:53.489
The best you're going to get is "Man, he's
almost as good as Norman."

01:31:53.489 --> 01:31:54.920
That's the best you can hope for.

01:31:54.920 --> 01:31:57.600
You will never be better than Norman, right?

01:31:57.600 --> 01:31:59.600
He's already conquered that hill.

01:31:59.600 --> 01:32:02.510
Go find another hill and make it your own.

01:32:02.510 --> 01:32:07.780
You can take pieces of Norman Rockwell, but
you can't be that exact same look.

01:32:07.780 --> 01:32:11.260
So I was putting all these different looks
together and then coming up with some crazy

01:32:11.260 --> 01:32:13.590
stuff and just making the spider part of it.

01:32:13.590 --> 01:32:15.880
The eyes got bigger, more webs.

01:32:15.880 --> 01:32:21.159
I had reinvented the webbing, I made the blue
a little bit darker, I forgot about anatomy.

01:32:21.159 --> 01:32:26.280
And I put them in these cool funky poses that
the readers just went crazy for.

01:32:26.280 --> 01:32:31.590
And every single time I walked into the offices,
so I didn't go there that often, they would

01:32:31.590 --> 01:32:36.270
call me on the carpet and they would say,
"No, no, Stop it.

01:32:36.270 --> 01:32:37.270
Stop it."

01:32:37.270 --> 01:32:40.400
And Tom denies it, but I'm like, "Tom, there
are moments of clarity in my life.

01:32:40.400 --> 01:32:41.780
This is one of them."

01:32:41.780 --> 01:32:44.270
Tom DeFalco was the editor in chief.

01:32:44.270 --> 01:32:45.480
He's an Italian guy.

01:32:45.480 --> 01:32:49.960
And he was giving me heck again, wiggling
his finger, going, "You've got to stop doing

01:32:49.960 --> 01:32:51.820
the big eyes."

01:32:51.820 --> 01:32:53.380
And then he got so mad.

01:32:53.380 --> 01:32:59.260
I remember his face getting a little red and
he goes, "And that webbing, those damn spaghetti

01:32:59.260 --> 01:33:01.620
webbing, you've got to stop it."

01:33:01.620 --> 01:33:05.540
Now from my perspective, ladies and gentlemen,
if you're in my head, it's like a Charlie

01:33:05.540 --> 01:33:06.790
Brown sort of cartoon.

01:33:06.790 --> 01:33:10.640
All I heard was "Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah,
spaghetti webbing."

01:33:10.640 --> 01:33:14.020
And I went, "Oh, my gosh, I've got a name
for them now."

01:33:14.020 --> 01:33:18.260
So I was so happy in that moment because Tom
gave me an official name for 'em.

01:33:18.260 --> 01:33:20.570
They've been known ever since as the spaghetti
webbing.

01:33:20.570 --> 01:33:22.080
Thank you, Tom DeFalco.

01:33:22.080 --> 01:33:25.929
I think he was cussing me out and giving me
heck at that point.

01:33:25.929 --> 01:33:29.659
I wasn't paying attention, though, because
I was like, "Oh, super cool, I've got a name

01:33:29.659 --> 01:33:30.860
for it."

01:33:30.860 --> 01:33:34.650
He then says in that same meeting, "Todd,
you've just got to control this stuff."

01:33:34.650 --> 01:33:42.080
And my answer was, and if anybody is under
the age of 30ish listening, I'm going to give

01:33:42.080 --> 01:33:44.290
you a bit of a golden rule.

01:33:44.290 --> 01:33:47.840
Anybody asks you to do something, especially
somebody in authority, always say yes even

01:33:47.840 --> 01:33:48.930
if you're not going to do it.

01:33:48.930 --> 01:33:50.730
It's just way easier.

01:33:50.730 --> 01:33:52.550
You get out of the room faster.

01:33:52.550 --> 01:33:56.960
No confrontation, just nod your head yes in
agreement and go do whatever the hell you

01:33:56.960 --> 01:33:57.960
want.

01:33:57.960 --> 01:34:03.830
And I knew that the editors would only circle
back every 90 days and look at the books and

01:34:03.830 --> 01:34:05.810
do their evaluation.

01:34:05.810 --> 01:34:07.780
And I walked out of that room.

01:34:07.780 --> 01:34:11.850
Not only did I not make the webbing smaller,
I'll show you the issue they got twice as

01:34:11.850 --> 01:34:12.850
long, right?

01:34:12.850 --> 01:34:14.330
Because they just look cool.

01:34:14.330 --> 01:34:15.330
I've got to tell you, Tim, they look cool.

01:34:15.330 --> 01:34:16.330
And by the time I came back, the next time
they —

01:34:16.330 --> 01:34:18.090
Tim Ferriss: I've seen the books, I've seen
them, I've seen them.

01:34:18.090 --> 01:34:19.090
I have them at home in Long Island where I
grew up..

01:34:19.090 --> 01:34:21.540
Todd McFarlane: They wiggle their finger at
you and they go, "No."

01:34:21.540 --> 01:34:24.239
And next time I go to New York, they'll go,
"No, Todd."

01:34:24.239 --> 01:34:28.860
But here's what was happening, and here was
their conundrum.

01:34:28.860 --> 01:34:32.530
Sales were going up, sales were going up.

01:34:32.530 --> 01:34:36.690
And at one point, again, I had that conversation.

01:34:36.690 --> 01:34:41.020
It's like, "Tom, what do you care how I draw?

01:34:41.020 --> 01:34:42.449
What do you care?

01:34:42.449 --> 01:34:47.050
All you should care about is that I am selling
you comic book.

01:34:47.050 --> 01:34:53.960
And you gave me the task of moving Spider-Man,
Amazing Spider-Man, from 21 up the ranks,

01:34:53.960 --> 01:34:55.640
and it's at number two right now."

01:34:55.640 --> 01:34:58.210
Jim Lee and The X-Men were beating us.

01:34:58.210 --> 01:34:59.630
And I was at one meeting, it was odd.

01:34:59.630 --> 01:35:01.080
There was this stranger in the room.

01:35:01.080 --> 01:35:02.850
I never met him before, didn't know who he
was.

01:35:02.850 --> 01:35:04.440
And he just sat there silent the whole time.

01:35:04.440 --> 01:35:05.590
He had this big, fat book.

01:35:05.590 --> 01:35:06.730
I didn't know who he was.

01:35:06.730 --> 01:35:12.210
And then finally at the end of an hour conversation
when I said that, I go, "I'm selling more

01:35:12.210 --> 01:35:15.000
books than almost anybody you employ right
here, right now."

01:35:15.000 --> 01:35:17.530
This dude, I found out later, was an accountant.

01:35:17.530 --> 01:35:20.130
He opened up his big, giant accounting book.

01:35:20.130 --> 01:35:21.800
Tom came behind him.

01:35:21.800 --> 01:35:26.960
The accountant didn't utter a word, he just
pointed at something on his data sheet and

01:35:26.960 --> 01:35:28.820
he shook his head yes.

01:35:28.820 --> 01:35:33.070
What he just said, "Yes, sales are going up."

01:35:33.070 --> 01:35:36.409
And it was, you could just see that it was
like, "What do we do?

01:35:36.409 --> 01:35:37.409
Right?

01:35:37.409 --> 01:35:42.540
It's working, but we disagree with it because
the status quo is getting, "Let me tell your

01:35:42.540 --> 01:35:43.540
audience."

01:35:43.540 --> 01:35:48.240
Here's the bizarre thing now that years have
passed.

01:35:48.240 --> 01:35:53.869
Everything they told me not to do on Spider-Man,
that I was rebellious against and I just stuck

01:35:53.869 --> 01:35:58.240
to my guns and I did it and the sales are
— do you know that if you're a young person

01:35:58.240 --> 01:36:03.530
right now and you go to Marvel and you draw
Spider-Man, do you know what style you have

01:36:03.530 --> 01:36:05.130
to draw Spider-Man in?

01:36:05.130 --> 01:36:07.670
Todd McFarlane.

01:36:07.670 --> 01:36:13.773
So the guy who was told not to create it,
I've now bizarrely, as I was going, "No, I'm

01:36:13.773 --> 01:36:15.710
not going to draw that status quo.

01:36:15.710 --> 01:36:18.400
I'm going to do something funky."

01:36:18.400 --> 01:36:21.300
My style is now the new status quo.

01:36:21.300 --> 01:36:26.290
And I don't think anybody should draw in Todd
McFarlane's style because the next person,

01:36:26.290 --> 01:36:31.150
they should be encouraging to do their thing
because it might be five times better than

01:36:31.150 --> 01:36:32.360
what I ever came up with.

01:36:32.360 --> 01:36:40.900
I don't understand corporations of just coming
up with an idea and glomming onto it so hard.

01:36:40.900 --> 01:36:43.680
Yes, I'm talking to you, IBM.

01:36:43.680 --> 01:36:48.550
And then these little dudes in the garage
come up with this little computer and they

01:36:48.550 --> 01:36:49.550
call it an Apple.

01:36:49.550 --> 01:36:57.720
And somehow they beat you eventually because
you become dinosaurs.

01:36:57.720 --> 01:37:05.420
And this is the thing, there is nobody in
the world that's ever made change and everybody

01:37:05.420 --> 01:37:12.130
like them, especially the people who had the
power and the prestige and the money ahead

01:37:12.130 --> 01:37:13.130
of them.

01:37:13.130 --> 01:37:17.770
Nobody, If you go to any corporation and you
say, "I've got this new idea," you will never

01:37:17.770 --> 01:37:22.980
hear the words from the people that are the
industry leaders, "That sounds super cool.

01:37:22.980 --> 01:37:28.159
Let us get out of your way so you can just
do that on your own unfettered."

01:37:28.159 --> 01:37:29.489
Are you out of your mind?

01:37:29.489 --> 01:37:35.540
They will take out bazookas and blades and
put down the strips and the throwing darts

01:37:35.540 --> 01:37:40.409
and they will do everything in their powers
to discourage you because they are industry

01:37:40.409 --> 01:37:41.409
leaders.

01:37:41.409 --> 01:37:45.860
But eventually, they become their own worst
enemy.

01:37:45.860 --> 01:37:50.119
Tim Ferriss: Todd, we are at almost two hours
now.

01:37:50.119 --> 01:37:53.650
I've realized that we've barely scratched
the service.

01:37:53.650 --> 01:37:58.489
We've established a lot of the background;
of course the personality, the rule breaking,

01:37:58.489 --> 01:38:00.880
the camel bladder.

01:38:00.880 --> 01:38:05.460
And we have not even touched upon your personal
relationship with Stan Lee, which is of great

01:38:05.460 --> 01:38:06.460
interest to me.

01:38:06.460 --> 01:38:09.449
We have not talked about the toy empire.

01:38:09.449 --> 01:38:11.949
We have not talked about how any of that started.

01:38:11.949 --> 01:38:14.430
TV, film, music, Spawn.

01:38:14.430 --> 01:38:18.280
I mean there's a long list of things that
I would love to cover with you.

01:38:18.280 --> 01:38:21.250
Would you be open to doing a round two?

01:38:21.250 --> 01:38:24.920
I think people would certainly be interested
in listening to one.

01:38:24.920 --> 01:38:26.880
Could I convince you to come back for a round
two?

01:38:26.880 --> 01:38:32.320
Todd McFarlane: Tim, you'll find that I'm
not shy at opening my mouth and talking.

01:38:32.320 --> 01:38:34.550
To the point I'm always going, "Am I boring
people?"

01:38:34.550 --> 01:38:38.070
Because I actually know all these stories,
because I lived them.

01:38:38.070 --> 01:38:45.770
But yeah, I think there are some interesting
forks in the road that may be not interesting

01:38:45.770 --> 01:38:50.889
for my career, but just sort of the human
condition of what happens when you get to

01:38:50.889 --> 01:38:51.889
certain wall.

01:38:51.889 --> 01:39:00.110
And now I've been talking about what I had
to do in one industry, but now because of

01:39:00.110 --> 01:39:05.650
that success, what you just mentioned, I was
able to break into multiple industries and

01:39:05.650 --> 01:39:15.950
found some of the same sort of repetition
and how you navigate the sharks when you're

01:39:15.950 --> 01:39:17.130
a guppy, right?

01:39:17.130 --> 01:39:18.700
So yeah, I'll come back.

01:39:18.700 --> 01:39:19.840
I appreciate you.

01:39:19.840 --> 01:39:22.349
Hopefully we haven't bored people, these two.

01:39:22.349 --> 01:39:24.300
Because they'll go, "Why would I want to listen
to another two?"

01:39:24.300 --> 01:39:27.119
So it's your show, I'll let you decide whether
that works.

01:39:27.119 --> 01:39:34.650
Tim Ferriss: Well ultimately, I mean let's
call it self-interested, if I keep it interesting

01:39:34.650 --> 01:39:40.699
for me, just like you in those 10-, 12-hour
days, you've got to keep your artwork interesting

01:39:40.699 --> 01:39:41.699
to you.

01:39:41.699 --> 01:39:44.050
Because otherwise, and even maybe still, it
can be really lonely.

01:39:44.050 --> 01:39:47.610
So for me, I just try to scratch my own itch
and asking questions about the things I'm

01:39:47.610 --> 01:39:48.610
interested in.

01:39:48.610 --> 01:39:49.610
So I'm very interested.

01:39:49.610 --> 01:39:52.260
I'm sure we'll have plenty of people along
for the ride.

01:39:52.260 --> 01:39:55.530
And people can find you on all the social
handles that I mentioned, of course.

01:39:55.530 --> 01:39:57.980
Are there any other places you'd like to point
them?

01:39:57.980 --> 01:40:00.610
They can find you on Instagram @ToddMcFarlane,
Twitter, @Todd_McFarlane.

01:40:00.610 --> 01:40:01.610
Todd McFarlane: Whatever.

01:40:01.610 --> 01:40:02.820
People they type it, you can find it.

01:40:02.820 --> 01:40:03.820
These are hipsters, right?

01:40:03.820 --> 01:40:04.820
I'm the old guy.

01:40:04.820 --> 01:40:05.820
Tim Ferriss: They'll type in your name.

01:40:05.820 --> 01:40:06.820
They'll find you.

01:40:06.820 --> 01:40:08.469
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, it's whatever people,
if you're interested you can find it.

01:40:08.469 --> 01:40:12.619
What I'll try and endeavor the next time is
to answer more than three questions.

01:40:12.619 --> 01:40:14.090
Because I think that's all you got in.

01:40:14.090 --> 01:40:18.469
I need to temper and get like, "Todd, he just
asked you how old you are.

01:40:18.469 --> 01:40:23.800
You don't have to talk about the entire evolution
of humanity to get to that answer."

01:40:23.800 --> 01:40:30.550
But I think that a little bit of backstory
to get to the reasoning why when you make

01:40:30.550 --> 01:40:32.599
that call at that moment matters.

01:40:32.599 --> 01:40:34.889
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah.

01:40:34.889 --> 01:40:35.889
It's critical.

01:40:35.889 --> 01:40:36.889
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

01:40:36.889 --> 01:40:39.239
So we've now painted, hopefully, some of the
personality.

01:40:39.239 --> 01:40:46.560
So now we can just maybe be a little more
varied into questions, and we can pepper and

01:40:46.560 --> 01:40:47.870
jump around a bunch of industries.

01:40:47.870 --> 01:40:51.409
And I can tell you some silly stories about
those ones too.

01:40:51.409 --> 01:40:55.949
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, we'll get into the trenches
and we can hear more of your art of war stories.

01:40:55.949 --> 01:40:58.780
Todd McFarlane: Right.The day I almost killed
Eddie Vedder.

01:40:58.780 --> 01:40:59.900
We'll talk about that one.

01:40:59.900 --> 01:41:00.900
Tim Ferriss: There we go.

01:41:00.900 --> 01:41:02.070
So that'll be the cliffhanger.

01:41:02.070 --> 01:41:06.969
And everybody listening, as usual, we will
put show notes and links to everything in

01:41:06.969 --> 01:41:11.330
the show notes at tim.blog/podcast.

01:41:11.330 --> 01:41:16.159
And until next time, don't be afraid of rocking
the boat and consider your upside downside.

01:41:16.159 --> 01:41:22.630
Just like you were talking about those artists
earlier and image, it's human nature, what

01:41:22.630 --> 01:41:23.630
a thing.

01:41:23.630 --> 01:41:25.260
And Todd, thank you for making the time today.

01:41:25.260 --> 01:41:27.770
So to be continued and we'll figure out a
time for round two.

01:41:27.770 --> 01:41:28.660
Todd McFarlane: All right, thank you, Tim.

